{"entity": "Phase Change Material", "type": "artifact", "description": "Phase Change Material (PCM) is a class of functional materials capable of storing and releasing large amounts of thermal energy as latent heat during phase transitions—most commonly between solid and liquid states—while maintaining a nearly constant temperature. PCMs encompass organic, inorganic, hydrated salt, fatty acid, and eutectic variants, with examples including paraffin wax, paraffin oil, palm wax, RT27, RT75, and composite formulations such as PCM-B-CRT27; a eutectic inorganic-organic PCM exhibits a melting range of 95.25–105.04°C and a specific heat capacity of 9.47 J/g.

In buildings, PCMs are integrated into envelopes, walls, roofs, glazing, facades, and roofing systems for dynamic heat storage and release, providing thermal buffering, reducing cooling loads, and improving comfort and energy efficiency. They are implemented in encapsulated, shape-stabilized, and macro-/micro-encapsulated forms, as well as in 3D-printed concrete, cement-lime mortars, gypsum, and porous calcium silicate panels. In cement-lime pastes, PCM reduces thermal conductivity and open porosity while substantially enhancing latent heat storage (e.g., melting point 23°C). In 3D-printed partitions, PCM content and form significantly enhance heat accumulation, though effects emerge only beyond a concentration threshold; cost remains a barrier to adoption in hot-humid climates.

In photovoltaics, PCMs provide passive cooling in PV-PCM, PVT, and BIPVT systems, absorbing excess heat during melting to regulate temperature and mitigate efficiency losses (e.g., reducing cell temperature by 4.3 K when used alone; a 307 K melting point can cause elevated nighttime temperatures during solidification). Paraffin-based PCM packs attached to BIPVT modules stabilize panel temperature and store recovered thermal energy, although direct attachment to frameless curtain-wall modules reduced thermal performance by 14.2% in one study.

In electronics thermal management, PCM-based heat sinks exploit latent heat absorption; typical properties include thermal conductivity of ~0.2 W/m·K, latent heat of ~200 kJ/kg, and densities of 860 (liquid) and ~900 (solid) kg/m³. PCMs can maintain device temperatures within ±5°C, lower maxima by 10–70°C, and extend thermal regulation periods. Melting behavior—modeled via enthalpy-porosity and lattice Boltzmann methods—involves buoyancy-driven natural convection, and fins, metal foams, and nanoparticles are employed to overcome inherently low thermal conductivity.

Further applications include battery thermal management (absorbing latent heat during discharge, improved with additives and encapsulation), cold storage and coolboxes for preserving food and fisheries products (pre-charged at −19°C and outperforming ice), solar drying systems enabling operation beyond sunlight hours, asphalt mixtures mitigating urban heat islands, temperature-controlled packaging, and micro-cold storage units. Separately, a distinct PCM class—exemplified by Ge2Sb2Te5—switches between amorphous and crystalline phases, with switching kinetics analyzed via Time-Temperature-Transformation diagrams; mixtures that phase-segregate upon crystallization are considered poor PCMs."}
{"entity": "OM-29 PCM", "type": "naturalobject", "description": "A phase change material used with an aluminum plate for PV cooling, resulting in a 10.35 °C temperature reduction and a 30% increase in power output."}
{"entity": "Photovoltaic Panel", "type": "artifact", "description": "A photovoltaic panel is a physical device designed to convert incident solar irradiance into useful electrical energy. Structurally, it typically consists of a rectangular panel featuring distinct front and back surfaces, often characterized by a grid of dark blue photovoltaic cells. Standardized physical dimensions for these panels can include a width of 100 cm and a height of 165 cm. The maximum electrical power output and overall performance of a photovoltaic panel are fundamentally characterized by the P_max equation, with electrical conversion efficiency serving as the primary metric for evaluation. Variations of these panels include 250 W-rated modules, as well as customized research apparatuses that may feature a large, flat brownish-tan surface enclosed within a white structural frame specifically designed to monitor performance metrics.

The electrical efficiency and power output of a photovoltaic panel are intrinsically linked to its operating conditions, which are frequently evaluated under varying configurations and meteorological factors. The panels are often deployed with an adjustable tilt angle to optimize exposure to the sun. However, performance can be negatively affected by environmental factors such as dust accumulation and elevated operating temperatures. Because increased heat hinders electrical output, thermal management is a critical focus of study. Various active and passive cooling techniques are employed to decrease cell temperature. Active cooling methods, such as water-cooled systems applied to tilted panels, have been successfully utilized to boost performance. Passively, wind-induced convection naturally cools the panel, a dynamic influenced by the panel's characteristic length in the flow direction.

To further enhance thermal regulation and efficiency, photovoltaic panels are frequently studied in advanced research applications, such as their integration with phase change materials (PCM). In these experimental setups, standard photovoltaic panels often serve as reference modules to compare against PV-PCM configurations under various tilt conditions. These combined cooling and monitoring strategies ensure that the photovoltaic panel mitigates the negative impacts of dust and heat, thereby maintaining optimal operating temperatures and maximizing its electrical output.<SEP>The device cooled by the PCM-based cooling system in the optimization framework; its energy-bound violations are quantified by the objective J_cv,d.<SEP>Solar photovoltaic (PV) panels that are the target of thermal management, with PCM-based cooling applied to them in case studies comparing passive versus active cooling configurations.<SEP>Solar energy device subject to temperature-related efficiency losses, mitigated through cooling strategies such as passive cooling and PCM-based thermal management.<SEP>Solar energy device converting sunlight into electricity; referenced studies apply cooling techniques and phase change materials to improve its thermal regulation, cooling, and electrical efficiency."}
{"entity": "Enhancing PV Panel Performance With Different Types Of Phase Change Material (PCM): An Experimental Study", "type": "content", "description": "A research paper presenting an experimental study on PV panel cooling using phase change materials, with Qusay Kamil Jasim as the corresponding author."}
{"entity": "PV Panel", "type": "artifact", "description": "A PV Panel, commonly known as a photovoltaic or solar panel, is a device designed to absorb solar energy and generate electricity through photovoltaic conversion. Structurally, it consists of multiple layers that capture solar irradiation, typically featuring a front glass surface and a back aluminum surface designed to dissipate heat to the ambient air. Physically, an experimental PV Panel often presents a dark blue, grid-textured top surface with specific dimensions of 300 mm in width and 700 mm in length. Commercial iterations of these panels typically exhibit Fill Factor values ranging from 0.70 to 0.85. 

In experimental and operational settings, the PV Panel frequently serves as a standard, conventional reference module. It is utilized to establish a baseline performance metric for comparing electrical efficiency and power output against enhanced PV-PCM (Phase Change Material) panels. To measure solar radiation and temperature distribution accurately, the reference panels are often tested in pairs under identical meteorological conditions. These panels are installed outside facing south to maximize solar exposure and are evaluated at various adjustable tilt angles—specifically 10°, 20°, 30°, and 40°. The overarching studies also evaluate these reference baselines against varying PCM thicknesses and cooling fin geometries to determine optimal thermal regulation strategies.

The performance of the conventional, ambient air-cooled PV Panel is defined by several key operational metrics. Under specific experimental conditions involving a 40° tilt angle and 841 W/m² of solar radiation, the reference panel achieves a maximum electrical efficiency of 13.5%. Operating under these exact conditions, the panel yields a maximum power output of 190.3 W. Across varying operational parameters within the same studies, the reference panel consistently registers an average power output of 165 W, while specific experimental models are generally rated at approximately 250 W of power.

Because elevated operating temperatures negatively impact the PV Panel's electrical efficiency and overall performance, managing its thermal behavior is a primary focus of ongoing research. As the panel's surface receives solar irradiation, it undergoes simultaneous photovoltaic conversion and thermal heating. During experimental comparisons, the reference panel's average surface temperature has been recorded at 39.18°C in numerical simulations and 40.92°C in laboratory experiments. To mitigate excessive heat and optimize electrical output, researchers actively analyze various cooling methods. By comparing the standard ambient-cooled PV Panel against systems integrating Phase Change Materials, researchers can evaluate the effectiveness of enhanced heat dissipation techniques, ensuring the panel's temperature is effectively managed and its electricity generation capabilities are maximized.<SEP>A photovoltaic panel with dimensions of 650 mm x 350 mm x 25 mm, used as the base component in the systems.<SEP>A conventional photovoltaic panel used as a reference system without PCM in the comparative study.<SEP>Photovoltaic panels that are subjected to cooling using thermal management systems.<SEP>Photovoltaic module, an example hot device cooled by the hybrid loop under pulsed or cyclic thermal loads.<SEP>Photovoltaic panel; its efficiency can be improved through PCM-based thermal management, and it is the subject of cooling case studies (passive and active with heat exchanger) in the surrounding research paper.<SEP>5cm thick photovoltaic panel cooled via a channel loaded with gravel (porous media) acting as cooling medium beneath it"}
