{"entity": "Phase Change Material (PCM)", "type": "concept", "description": "Phase Change Material (PCM) is a class of functional substances that store and release large amounts of thermal energy as latent heat during reversible phase transitions—typically melting and crystallization—while remaining nearly isothermal at the melting temperature T_m. Key thermophysical parameters include specific heat capacity, latent heat of fusion (defining the Stefan number and total enthalpy), liquid fraction from 0 (fully solid) to 1 (fully liquid), and ~10–15% volumetric expansion upon melting. Their main disadvantage is characteristically low thermal conductivity, motivating enhancement via nanoparticles, metal foams, and fins; in paraffin-based nanocomposites, thermal behavior also depends on nanoparticle dispersion, viscosity, and thermal cycles. Representative materials include paraffin wax, P21, OM42, OM55, RT21-HC, CrodaTherm 19/21, bio-based esters E19/E21, and eutectic KCl solutions.

Acting as latent thermal batteries, PCMs absorb heat nearly isothermally while melting—excess energy from power generation or daytime solar gains—and release it while solidifying, heating air or a coolant when generation is insufficient or sunlight declines, thereby clipping peak temperatures. They form hybrid composites with porous high-silica zeolites such as clinoptilolite (50% loading optimal in one study) and enter lime mortars as microencapsulated slurries or powders; multi-PCM systems (melting points of 18 °C and 25 °C at 20% total dosage) yield controllable thermal activation profiles, though PCM addition raises mortar GWP by ~25–35% and dominates raw-material costs, making selection environmentally and economically critical.

Applications include passive cooling and thermal regulation in building envelopes, facades, glazing, roofs, foamed cement, vacuum-impregnated mortars, and additively manufactured components (melting points 19.8–29.3 °C), with recurring annual savings offsetting high initial investment (UAE glazing: $6.35/kg installed, 35-year lifespan), though roof systems with incomplete overnight solidification achieved only 62% exploitation. PCMs manage lithium-ion pouch-cell battery temperatures in electric vehicles; electronics heat sinks buffer transients, hold devices within ±5 °C, and lower maxima 10–70 °C below ~50 °C thresholds. Further uses include cold-chain fish preservation, pavement subgrade stabilization against frost heave and thaw weakening, and frost-resistant concrete containing PCM-filled steel balls. In active photovoltaic cooling, hot coolant deposits energy into the PCM—potentially the sole cooling path during heat-exchanger bypass—while PV/BIPVT integration damps panel heating and stabilizes electrical output despite minor rear-panel-attachment penalties. In photonics, Sb₂S₃ (melting point 823 K) enables reversible phase switching for SLM/metasurface applications; key design variables (heat capacity, melt temperature, stored energy, mass, and energy bounds) support optimization extendable to batteries and power electronics.

In solar distillation—often termed the "PCM method"—PCM provides latent-heat thermal energy storage: it absorbs solar energy during peak midday radiation via its melting enthalpy and gradually releases the stored latent heat in afternoon/evening hours as radiation declines. This stabilizes feed-water temperature, sustains higher water temperatures and stable operation, extends distillate production duration, and raises solar-to-distillate energy conversion efficiency, significantly enhancing nighttime performance. In specific implementations, the PCM was an orange-colored rectangular block placed within the internal copper trough of one distillation unit, and beeswax served as the PCM in one study. Tubular solar stills with PCM sustained higher freshwater production than non-PCM conditions; quantitatively, daily productivity improved by 11–13% and total system efficiency by 11.47–13.9%.

Notably, the acronym PCM also denotes phase change memory, a distinct technology in which chalcogenide compounds switch via rhombohedral-cubic structural transitions, which should not be confused with the thermal storage material. PCM melting dynamics and novel sustainable PCMs remain active research subjects.<SEP>Materials with high latent heat storage capacity and phase change properties that allow efficient thermal energy absorption and release, making them attractive alternatives to conventional cooling solutions for electronics; examples investigated include RT35, Paraffin Wax, and Salt Hydrate.<SEP>A material that transitions between solid and liquid states, absorbing heat energy as it melts when temperature rises and releasing energy back to the environment as it solidifies when temperature falls, utilizing latent heat for temperature regulation.<SEP>A class of thermal storage materials characterized by low intrinsic thermal conductivity, used in thermal energy storage and electronics cooling applications."}
{"entity": "PCM-PV Integration", "type": "concept", "description": "The combination of phase change materials with photovoltaic panels to control surface temperatures."}
{"entity": "Photovoltaic Module", "type": "artifact", "description": "The Photovoltaic Module is a solar energy conversion device that generates electricity from incident sunlight. Its electrical performance, power output, and conversion efficiency are highly dependent on operating parameters, most notably temperature. A defining thermal characteristic of the photovoltaic module is that often more than 70% of the incident solar spectrum is absorbed as heat rather than converted into electrical work, meaning that its energy distribution and thermal losses must be actively managed to maintain system stability. Under high irradiation, the module's temperature can exceed optimal efficiency thresholds, which degrades its conversion efficiency and reduces its daily energy yield.

In a PCM-based cooling optimization case study, the Photovoltaic Module serves as the "hot device." Within this case study, it is characterized by an energy state E_d, a thermal capacitance C_d of 4580 J/°C, an absorptivity of 0.7, a surface area A_s of 0.8 m², and a conversion efficiency of approximately 20%. Cooling the module lowers its operating temperature and increases its daily energy production.

The thermal regulation of the Photovoltaic Module is the central target of phase change material (PCM)-based cooling optimization strategies. Cited studies integrate photovoltaic modules with phase change materials and apply hybrid approaches, such as finned-PCM configurations and nanoparticle-enhanced PCM, to achieve high-efficiency passive cooling. These PCM-based thermal management techniques enhance both the module's electricity yield and its operational lifespan. In economic evaluations, conventional bare photovoltaic modules serve as the benchmark against which the levelized cost of electricity (LCOE) of cooled modules is compared."}
{"entity": "PCM Cooling System", "type": "artifact", "description": "A phase change material cooling system integrated with PV cells to reduce PV cell temperature by up to 10 K using internal fins, thereby maintaining higher PV conversion efficiency and increasing electrical power output.<SEP>A cooling system designed to regulate the operating cell temperature of the photovoltaic module via Phase Change Material to enhance electrical efficiency.<SEP>A system integrating Phase Change Materials to prevent PV panels from overheating, managing temperature and leading to efficiency improvements.<SEP>A cooling system based on Phase Change Material, forming the subject of the study in which Heat Sink A serves as a key component.<SEP>Cooling system built around PCM; the generic power flow functions can express more advanced internal relationships of the PCM cooling system including additional dynamics.<SEP>Phase change material cooling system whose optimization is the subject of the paper; includes a heat exchanger loop that influences PCM phase-change behavior.<SEP>The cooling system based on Phase Change Material whose design and control are optimized in the paper; its states and control inputs are presented in Fig. 4."}
{"entity": "PV/PCM System", "type": "artifact", "description": "A system developed by Li et al. by attaching PCM to the back of a PV module, reducing PV temperature by up to 23°C compared to a PV-only setup and resulting in a 5.18% increase in electricity output.<SEP>A combined photovoltaic and phase change material system whose benefits, limitations, and economic considerations are discussed in the review.<SEP>A system integrating a photovoltaic module with a phase change material storage system to maintain temperature within a desirable range.<SEP>A photovoltaic/phase change material system evaluated for its thermal performance and phase-change dynamics."}
{"entity": "Photovoltaic Panels", "type": "artifact", "description": "Photovoltaic panels are solar energy conversion devices designed to convert sunlight directly into electricity. They typically appear as large, rectangular panels featuring dark blue, grid-like surfaces characteristic of silicon cells, and are commonly mounted side-by-side on A-frame stands. As devices whose electrical efficiency is routinely calculated and analyzed—including the quantification of associated uncertainty—photovoltaic panels serve as central subjects of experimental and analytical research.

The electrical efficiency and overall performance of photovoltaic panels are heavily influenced by their operating temperatures, which makes them a primary focus of passive cooling research and the target of various cooling techniques aimed at improving electrical output. Both the efficiency and longevity of these panels can be enhanced through active cooling that reduces their operating temperatures, and they also function as an application area for hybrid thermal management strategies based on PCM-driven active cooling approaches.

A prominent method of thermal management involves the integration of Phase Change Materials (PCMs). In specialized PV-PCM systems, the surface temperature of photovoltaic panels is actively regulated by the PCM to prevent overheating, effectively managing and reducing the peak temperatures reached during operation, thereby enhancing operational efficiency. These panels have been experimentally tested both with and without PCM integration across various tilt angles and solar radiation intensities to evaluate performance differences. Notably, the application of NePCMs (nano-enhanced Phase Change Materials) has been shown to substantially boost panel performance, resulting in increased power output and improved overall energy efficiency.<SEP>Solar energy conversion devices whose performance is improved through cooling techniques, including phase change material based thermal regulation and various other cooling methods."}
{"entity": "PV-PCM (Photovoltaic-Phase Change Material) Systems", "type": "concept", "description": "Systems combining photovoltaic panels with phase change materials for temperature reduction, serving as the core subject of the referenced research."}
{"entity": "Efficiency", "type": "concept", "description": "A measure of photovoltaic cell performance that decreases as temperature increases, specifically losing 0.45% per degree above 25°C for crystalline silicon.<SEP>The performance metric of the PV panels aimed to be enhanced by integrating Phase Change Materials.<SEP>A performance metric used to compare the conventional PV panel and the PV-PCM system, showing an increase of 3.8% to 6.5% with the use of PCM.<SEP>Ratio of energy absorbed by the system to energy used for water evaporation, computed from Qin and Qout using equation 3; the percentage improvement due to PCM is calculated with equation 4."}
{"entity": "RT68", "type": "naturalobject", "description": "A specific type of paraffin wax phase change material used in the study.<SEP>A specific type of Phase Change Material experimentally implemented for PV panel cooling.<SEP>A type of phase change material (PCM) tested for PV panel cooling<SEP>A specific Phase Change Material tested in the study, which corresponds to PCM3 and showed superior performance.<SEP>A type of phase change material (PCM3) used in the current study's systematic testing, which when combined with optimal tilt angle (40°) yields performance gains exceeding prior work."}
{"entity": "Phase-change Materials", "type": "naturalobject", "description": "Phase-change materials are a class of functional materials characterized by their ability to switch between different structural states, most notably between crystalline and amorphous phases, a property underpinning various switching mechanisms. They are distinguished by a pronounced difference in optical reflectivity between these two states. Prominent examples include chalcogenide alloys such as Ge-Sb-Te, with specific compositions like GST-225 and GST-124 located along the pseudo-binary line, and layered chalcogenide compounds are also relevant to this class. These materials are generally covalently bonded and typically exhibit PME values around 20%, which is lower than the elevated PME observed in certain studied amorphous states. Phase-change materials have been actively investigated in materials science research, including by researchers such as B.J. Kooi and M. Wutig.

In electronics and photonics, phase-change materials are widely applied in data storage and memory technologies, where their reversible structural transitions are exploited in memory taxonomy and device operation. Their unusual optical properties also make them valuable in advanced photonics research, including metasurfaces and nanoantenna resonances.

Beyond electronics, phase-change materials serve important thermal management functions. They absorb or release heat when undergoing a phase transition at a nearly constant temperature, leveraging their latent heat capacity. This behavior enables their use in passive building design and facade integration, where they moderate temperature fluctuations through thermal buffering by absorbing and releasing heat. Additionally, they are employed to absorb excess heat from solar cells, reducing peak operating temperatures during the melting process.<SEP>Materials investigated to understand electronic switching mechanisms in memory devices"}
