Analysis: Figure 7 presents a multi-panel chart showing the temperature distribution across the front face of PV-PCM panels at a solar radiation intensity of 841 W/m². The chart is organized as four subplots, each corresponding to a different panel tilt angle (10°, 20°, 30°, and 40°). Within each subplot, temperature profiles are plotted for different PCM types (PCM1, PCM2, PCM3) along with the reference panel (no PCM), measured at nine thermocouple positions (T1 at the bottom to T9 at the top) along the panel surface. Key findings include: (1) all PCM types reduce surface temperature compared to the reference panel; (2) temperature distribution is non-uniform, with the top region (T9) consistently hotter than the bottom region (T1); (3) at PCM1, the top-to-bottom temperature differential increases with tilt angle — 16.5%, 18.2%, 19.1%, and 19.8% for 10°, 20°, 30°, and 40° respectively; (4) this non-uniformity arises because PCM melts and migrates upward in the containment box due to buoyancy effects, leaving the upper portion less effectively cooled. The chart is critical for understanding thermal management performance of different PCM materials and demonstrates that tilt angle and PCM type significantly influence temperature uniformity, which directly impacts electrical output (discussed in Figure 8), cell efficiency, and PV panel longevity."}
