{"entity": "Thermal Conductivity Enhancement", "type": "concept", "description": "Thermal Conductivity Enhancement denotes the improvement of a material's ability to conduct heat, and it constitutes a central research objective in the field of phase change materials (PCMs). It is regarded as a primary benefit and key goal of integrating nanotechnology into PCMs, and serves as the core theme of a review paper concerning methods for improving the thermal conductivity and broader heat-transfer effectiveness of PCMs—a theme addressed in the introduction, body, literature review, and conclusion sections of the host manuscript. The challenge of improving PCM thermal conductivity is notably pursued in connection with supporting material strategies, and it is treated both as a research topic and an experimental achievement, with claims supported by cited references such as reference [8] and the Ryms et al. citation.

