Quantitatively, the enhancement is expressed as a percentage improvement in thermal conductivity relative to pure paraffin wax, normalized in Figure 14, ranging from a minimum of 2.94% (achieved with 1% gamma-phase nanoparticles) to a maximum of 56.80% (achieved with 1% TiO2). The nanoparticle-induced enhancement is linked to a measured acceleration of the discharge process, indicated by the steepness of the solidification curves and the reduced time required to reach the completion threshold. In nano-PCM mixtures, the degree of enhancement is affected by nanoparticle particle size, and the improvement in heat conduction performance is further influenced by nanofluid stability.

As a performance aspect, Thermal Conductivity Enhancement has been validated in practical PCM-heat sink applications and represents a key strategy for improving the heat transfer performance of PCM-based systems in electronics cooling, thermal management, and aerospace electronics cooling. In the parent research paper, it is studied alongside the stability and homogeneity of nano-PCM systems, and a notable conclusion is that high thermal conductivity nanoparticles alone do not guarantee optimal PCM performance."}
