{"entity": "Phase-Change Material", "type": "concept", "description": "Phase-Change Material (PCM) denotes a class of functional materials characterized by reversible transitions between structural states—most commonly amorphous and crystalline phases, with liquid states also occurring depending on temperature—whose fluidity and viscosity follow an Arrhenius-type activation relation. Typical PCMs are chalcogenides such as GeTe, doped Sb₂Te, Ge₂Sb₂Te₅, and Ge-Sb-Te alloys like GST-124, while Sb₂S₃ is a widely used low-loss optical variant; In₂Te₃, by contrast, is not a phase-change material. PCMs also exist as form-stable silica-supported, microencapsulated slurry, and microencapsulated powder types, and paraffin-based materials such as paraffin oil and paraffin-wax composites serve as PCMs in thermal contexts.

In electronic memory, PCM is the functional storage material sandwiched between electrodes in PCRAM cells, crystallized by the SET operation and amorphized by the RESET operation. Switching is driven by Joule heat from electronic pulses or high current densities in nanometer-sized devices; melt-quenching requires rapid cooling of roughly 100 K/ns. Resistance changes by orders of magnitude: the amorphous state exhibits high, Arrhenius-governed resistance, while the crystalline state is metallic and retained irreversibly below the crystallization temperature. This resistivity contrast, often with strong reflectivity contrast, enables simple electrical and optical readout in non-volatile cells. PCMs exhibit threshold switching dependent on both time and applied field, crystallize within nanoseconds—with alloy tailoring and doping promising faster switching—and are regarded as a potential bridge technology that may compete with or complement DRAM while eliminating its refresh requirement.

In photonics, GST-124 exhibits gradual phase transitions under specific laser parameters during laser-induced amorphization, and crystal growth velocity in melt-quenched specimens has been studied. The refractive-index contrast between phases enables non-volatile, fast, reconfigurable wavelength and resonance tuning with broad C-band coverage in integrated photonic devices, waveguide Bragg gratings, external-cavity lasers (ECLs), and metasurfaces, offering an alternative to thermo-optic tuning. Electrical pulses can progressively modulate the crystallization fraction to vary the effective refractive index for wavelength switching, though heating may cause thermal crosstalk. Sb₂S₃, exploited in ECL passive waveguides and reconfigurable photonic devices for optical communication, enables functionality without continuous power consumption. Micro-heater architectures and inverse-designed heaters, with heater/substrate resistivity engineered via doping, induce transitions for optical modulation and emission control.

In thermal energy storage, PCMs act as thermal accumulators that store energy at the rate of cooling power supplied, with design capacity and melting temperature chosen by co-design formulations, storing and releasing heat through melting and solidification. Discharging rates are enhanced by combining fins and nanoparticles, applied in liquid desiccant air-conditioning for cooling and dehumidification; configurations include finned cubic cells monitored via liquid fraction and copper foam composites improving thermal regulation and conductivity. PCMs can be sputtered as thin films (e.g., 20 nm, crystallized by annealing) and structured at micrometer scales on pre-structured samples, though etching is unsuitable for top contacts since the material is weaker than titanium nitride; characterization includes van-der-Pauw measurements requiring accurate temperature control. Overall, phase-change materials constitute a versatile class central to non-volatile memory, reconfigurable photonics, and thermal management and energy storage."}
