{"reference_id": "8", "content": "A computational fluid dynamics analysis using Fluent software will be conducted for the PV-PCM system. The obtained results will be compared with the results of our previous laboratory experimental study [19]. In addition, the effects on the average PV panel surface temperature will be revealed by changing the initial ambient temperature and the amount of radiation.

Numerical analysis

In this section, the analysis of the PV-PCM container with rectangular horizontal flat fin geometry was carried out in two dimensions and compared with the results of the 60-minute laboratory experiment obtained from the previous study. View of the designed model in crosssection is given in Figure 1. PV module consists of (36cm x 42cm) mono-crystalline silicon cells. ANSYS Fluent 18.2 software was used to solve the momentum, heat conduction and heat diffusion equations. Solid mode and equations are shown in detail. The analysis was carried out with wind speed, the initial ambient temperature which are 0 m/s and $2 0 \ ^ { \circ } \mathsf { C } ,$ PV panel tilt angle of 90° and irradiance of 1200 W/m<sup>2</sup> as boundary conditions. The boundary conditions and mesh mode are schematically represented in Figure 2 (a) and 2 (b).

Solid model

The Navier-Stokes equation, which is the two-dimensional momentum equation of the model with fins, was calculated in the CFD software with the following formulas:

In the Navier-Stokes equation, the variable represented by ρ represents the fluid density $( \mathsf { k g } / \mathsf { m } ^ { 3 } ) ;$ u represents the x speed and v represents the y speed $( { \mathfrak { m } } / { \mathfrak { s } } ) ;$ g represents the gravitational acceleration (kg $\mathsf { m } / \mathsf { s } ^ { 2 } )$ and μ represents the PCM's viscosity $( \mathsf { N S } / \mathsf { m } ^ { 2 } )$ . As shown in Table 1, the PCM has the following thermophysical characteristics and aluminum has the following characteristics.

Modeling of heat transfer

As a result of a PV-PCM system, the PV panel cannot absorb heat and must transfer it to the PCM as waste heat, and the environment of the two-dimensional PV-PCM model is assumed to be adiabatic [20]. The heat transfer equations of the two-dimensional model are shown below:

The specific heat of each solid piece is shown as Cp and its unit is $\mathsf { J } / ( \mathsf { k g } \mathsf { K } )$ , and the thermal conductivity of each solid piece is shown as k and its unit is $\mathsf { W } / ( \mathsf { m } \mathsf { K } )$

PCM model

When PCM melts or freezes, the equations of solid-liquid phase change can be shown as solid, solid-liquid and liquid states as follows:

Here, PCM's liquid temperature is represented by Tl during melting-freezing; PCM temperature is represented by T while solid temperature is represented by Ts during melting-freezing.

Result and discussion

