{"entity": "PCM", "type": "naturalobject", "description": "PCM is an abbreviation referring to two distinct concepts: Phase Change Material and Phase Change Memory.

As a Phase Change Material, PCM denotes substances that store and release large latent heat at nearly constant temperature through reversible phase transitions, prized for isothermal behavior and high heat storage density. PCMs span gas-solid, gas-liquid, solid-solid, and solid-liquid types, classified as organic (paraffins, fatty acids, biobased esters), inorganic (salts, salt hydrates), eutectic, and polymeric (e.g., PEG), selected by melting range, latent heat, density, conductivity, and peak temperature; hysteresis is common. Paraffin PCMs melt from white semi-solids into dark amber translucent liquids. Challenges include low thermal conductivity (restricting heat flux and prolonging charge/discharge), leakage, supercooling, phase separation, volume change, low chemical and thermal stability, high flammability, and cycling degradation—mitigated by encapsulation, porous matrices, nanostructures, metal foams or fibers, silica or bio-based form-stable formats, and coated ceramic scaffolds; a ceramic-scaffold composite PCM supports aerospace electronics thermal management. Modeled as thermal batteries (capacity C_pcm = m_pcm·L_f), PCMs clip temperature peaks of hot devices, operating standalone or with heat exchangers.

Applications include PV-PCM systems absorbing panel waste heat by day and solidifying at night, cutting temperatures up to 42% and enhancing electrical efficiency; BIPVT paraffin packs marginally boosting electrical output while reducing thermal efficiency 14.2%; building envelopes damping daily oscillations (a 1 cm layer balancing cost/performance over 35 years); cementitious fillers lengthening setting and lowering conductivity, porosity, density, and strength; electronics heat sinks dropping temperatures 10.8/50.1/69.8 °C at 5/10/15 W, though capacity may exhaust after ~34 minutes; battery thermal management; cold storage (−19 °C PCM outperforming water and ice); waste-heat recovery; and latent-heat thermal energy storage, including PCM filling TES plate lattices and PCM-metal foam composites whose melting evolution is studied. Clinoptilolite-hydrocarbon composites (melting ~27–28 °C, enthalpy 190 J/g) enable thermoregulating textiles. In lime renders and mortars, PCM is dosed as form-stable, microencapsulated slurry, or powder (typically 20% of lime weight); powder types fail freeze-thaw catastrophically, and multi-PCM mortars enable multi-stage regulation. Nano-PCMs with nanoparticles (CuO, alumina, titania, silver, Fe3O4) show melting, charging, and discharging dependent on nanomaterial type, concentration, and cycling; hot PCM solidifies nearly isothermally (~70 °C to ~50 °C), with pure paraffin as benchmark.

In solar distillation, PCM acts as latent heat storage—absorbing energy under strong radiation to slow temperature rise and releasing heat gradually in afternoon and night—sustaining evaporation and freshwater production during cloudy or nighttime conditions, improving efficiency and productivity (66.18% and 54.04% in prior research); a 60–65 °C melting PCM (including beeswax) maintains TaPCM temperature (65 °C vs 60 °C at 15:00) after radiation declines.

Distinctly, PCM also abbreviates Phase Change Memory, a non-volatile memory technology also called PRAM and Ovonic Unified Memory. Fast-switching chalcogenide candidates are identified via thermodynamic analysis, with GST as the standard comparison material; these materials are electrically pulsed between amorphous and crystalline states, and wide-bandgap Sb2S3 enables large-area phase switching in reconfigurable photonics, including PCMs capped with 80 nm Al2O3 and crystallized by annealing and electrical switching.<SEP>Phase-change material whose heating involves substantial heat generation, requiring prevention of thermal crosstalk to the semiconductor SOA to minimize thermal interference with laser performance.<SEP>The phase-change material region of the photonic device, represented together with the external cavity by the upper rectangular structure in the thermal simulation."}
