{"entity1": "PCM", "entity2": "Thermal Conductivity", "description": "The PCM-Thermal Conductivity relation describes the relationship between phase change materials (PCMs) and thermal conductivity, a critical property governing their performance in thermal energy storage applications. PCMs inherently exhibit low thermal conductivity, with coefficients typically ranging from 0.2 to 0.7, which is widely recognized as their main limitation. This low conductivity constitutes a significant disadvantage in thermal storage processes and slows down the recovery of the PCM's initial solid state. Conversely, the effective heat release of the PCM during the thermal charge phase is attributed to its thermal conductivity, underscoring the importance of this property to overall system behavior. Accordingly, exploring PCMs with improved thermal conductivity is recommended as a means of enhancing system performance.

When characterizing this property, thermal conductivity is typically determined under two conditions corresponding to the solid and liquid states of the PCM, since the physical state of the material influences the measured values.

The effect of PCM incorporation on the thermal conductivity of host materials is context-dependent and varies by matrix type. In pastes, the addition of PCM reduces thermal conductivity, especially when the PCM is in its liquid state. In contrast, mortars containing PCMs show higher thermal conductivity than the corresponding reference mortars without PCMs; this increase is treated as a penalty in tradeoff analyses. These contrasting effects indicate that the PCM-Thermal Conductivity relationship differs across composite systems, with PCM addition lowering conductivity in paste systems while raising it in cementitious mortar systems.<SEP>Heat-transfer effectiveness of PCMs combined in heatsinks is investigated along with the thermal conductivity volume fraction."}
