{"entity1": "LWAM", "entity2": "PCM", "description": "The LWAM-PCM relation describes the interaction between LWAM (Lightweight Aggregate Mortar), a high-porosity mortar, and phase change materials (PCM), which are incorporated into the mortar to modify its thermal and mechanical properties. A central characteristic of this relation is LWAM's exceptional PCM absorption capacity: among the mortars studied, LWAM absorbs the highest mass and the largest absolute volume of PCM, with the highest volume also observed on average. This superior absorption is attributed to LWAM's high initial porosity, which enables it to accommodate a wide range of PCM viscosities. However, the absorbed PCM volume varies depending on the specific PCM type used.

In terms of thermal performance, the incorporation of PCM into LWAM produces the most evident effect among the studied mortars. When combined with PCM, LWAM achieves the highest specific heat while exhibiting minimum thermal conductivity. Furthermore, the high PCM absorption results in the most pronounced temperature stabilization observed in the study. These thermal behaviors were evaluated through transient thermal response testing, in which LWAM samples were examined both with and without PCM incorporation to compare their performance.

Regarding mechanical effects, PCM incorporation produces a substantial increase in the flexural strength of LWAM. This enhancement is attributed to two factors: LWAM's highest absorbed PCM mass among the studied mortars, and its initially lowest strength, which leaves greater room for improvement. Overall, the LWAM-PCM relation is characterized by maximal PCM uptake, enhanced thermal storage and insulation properties, improved temperature stabilization, and notable gains in flexural strength.<SEP>LWAM incorporates the greatest PCM volume, from 4.33% with P21 to 6.37% with E19, due to its larger average pore size and open-pore fraction."}
