{"reference_id": "8", "content": "These results confirm the effectiveness of PCM in reducing peak temperatures and stabilizing the thermal response of the building envelope, with positive effects on indoor comfort and reduction of the thermal load during the hottest hours.

The daily variation of Δτ calculated using equation 5 is shown in Figure 8 together with the average temperature trend of the core of the PCM-B-CRT27. The time lag indicates the delay between the peak external temperature and the peak internal surface temperature, providing a direct indicator of the PCM's ability to dampen and delay incoming heat flows compared to the reference case (Case A). During the month of June, Δτ shows marked daily variability, with values generally ranging between 1 h 45 min and 17 h. An exception is observed on June 4, when the peak internal temperature occurred earlier than the external peak, suggesting a temporal reversal in the thermal response of the envelope.

In the first few days of the month, the average temperature of the PCM core is below $2 7 ~ ^ { \circ } \mathrm { C }$ , indicating partial activation. Subsequently, the temperature rises until it stabilizes around $3 { \bar { 0 } } ^ { \circ } \mathrm { C } .$ , thus operating for most of the period within or just beyond the latent phase range (see Figure 5).

Overall, the results confirm that integrating PCM into the roofing system allows for effective thermal regulation, reducing peak temperatures, mitigating daily fluctuations, and improving the dynamic stability of the building envelope, with a positive impact on both indoor comfort and thermal load. Furthermore, the system developed, analyzed in previous laboratory-scale experimental studies by the authors [7,8], has demonstrated considerable potential for application in real-world contexts.

The monitoring period, conducted during June, corresponds to the onset of sustained overheating conditions in the attic space and therefore represents a critical phase for assessing PCM activation and discharge dynamics under realistic operating conditions. The experimental results highlight that the effectiveness of the PCM-integrated roofing system is governed by the interaction between the PCM phase transition range, the diurnal temperature amplitude, and the availability of night-time cooling potential. The observed progressive reduction in latent heat utilization after mid-June indicates that when night-time temperatures remain elevated, incomplete solidification may occur, reducing the capacity for daily thermal cycling. Under milder climatic conditions or during transitional seasons characterized by larger day–night temperature differences, improved solidification is expected, leading to enhanced latent heat exploitation and greater thermal buffering. Conversely, in persistently hot climates with limited nocturnal cooling, the system performance may progressively shift toward sensible heat storage behavior. These findings suggest that the applicability and optimal design of PCM-integrated roofing systems are climate-dependent and require alignment between the PCM melting temperature and the expected indoor operative temperature range. The experimental dataset obtained in this study therefore provides a validated reference for future modelling activities aimed at extending the assessment to different climatic contexts, seasonal conditions, and PCM configurations.

4 Conclusions

The study analyzed the performance of an innovative roofing system integrated with calcium silicate panels impregnated with PCM (PCM-B-CRT27), comparing it with a reference configuration without latent storage. The experimental results highlighted the effectiveness of PCM in reducing incoming heat flows, attenuating daily oscillations, and improving the dynamic stability of the system when the material operates within its phase change temperature range.

During the monitoring period, the PCM system achieved a monthly reduction in total heat gain of 23% compared to the reference case, accompanied by an average decrease in heat flow peaks of 55%. Significant reductions in internal surface temperature (up to 4.5 °C) and operating temperature (up to 1.8 °C) were also recorded, confirming the material's effective thermal damping capacity and improved indoor comfort conditions. Time lag analysis further confirmed the PCM-B-CRT27's ability to delay heat transfer, with Δτ values ranging from 1 h 45 min to 17 h, depending on operating conditions and the level of PCM activation.

At the same time, the experimental results clearly demonstrate that the effectiveness of the system is strongly dependent on the correct selection of the PCM melting temperature. During periods characterized by higher thermal loads, the core temperature of the PCM exceeded the upper limit of the phase change range, preventing complete solidification during night-time ventilation. Under these conditions, the PCM progressively lost its latent storage functionality and mainly contributed through sensible heat storage, resulting in a reduced capacity to attenuate heat flow peaks. This behavior highlights that an underestimated melting temperature limits the exploitation of latent heat and reduces the overall performance of the system, despite the presence of PCM.

The integration of PCM into the roofing system enabled effective passive control of heat flows, highlighting the system's potential for applications in real building contexts, but the results emphasize that PCM selection must be carefully tailored to the expected climatic conditions and operating temperature ranges. The choice of an appropriate melting temperature is therefore a key design parameter to ensure full activation of the latent storage potential and to maximize the benefits of PCM-based building envelope solutions.

Future studies will focus on validating the energy model based on the experimental data collected in this study and extending the analysis to different calcium silicate-PCM configurations with different types of PCM and melting temperatures. This approach will allow optimization of the system design and a more comprehensive evaluation of PCM effectiveness across different climates and operating scenarios.

