{"entity": "Fins", "type": "artifact", "description": "Fins are extended surfaces and physical components designed to increase the surface area available for heat transfer, thereby enhancing thermal conductivity and heat dissipation across a wide range of engineering and technological applications. Structurally, fins commonly appear as a dense array of parallel formations—horizontal or vertical—extending upward from the base plate of a finned metallic block, or as rectangular extended surfaces attached to the wall of an enclosure. A typical fin has normalized dimensions (l, h, w) = (63, 4, 8)/L, with its effective heating surface computed as S_H,FIN = 2(lw + lh + wh) − wh, excluding the contact surface with the wall. In thermal studies, fins are modeled as heated solid surfaces or rectangular heating elements protruding from the heated left wall into an enclosure and held at a prescribed fixed temperature T_H; their arrangement (multiple, single, or none) defines the heating configurations compared. When internal—for example, four fins within a cubic enclosure—their occupied volume is subtracted to obtain the PCM volume, V_PCM. Fins may take rectangular, square, or plate-like geometric arrangements, and their spacing influences thermal interference and melting performance.

Within latent heat thermal energy storage and phase change material (PCM) technologies, fins are solid heating elements protruding into the bulk of the PCM cell from the source boundary, offering a simple and effective means of enhancing overall thermal performance. Integrated with PCMs, they yield effective thermal conductivities 5–14 times greater than those of pure PCM and accelerate melting and solidification. Distributing the heating surface over multiple slender fins is more effective than a single plate-like element, with multiple-fin layouts exploiting interstitial spaces to melt strictly more material (positive ΔV_m) than single-fin or finless configurations of equal heating area—an advantage that holds particularly in the conduction-dominated regime. Heated liquid PCM rises along the fin surfaces, and multiple properly spaced fins enable more efficient melting at lower relative temperature via the onset of convection. Fins positioned in the lower parts of the chamber produce larger convective motions and enhanced thermal transfer, whereas improperly positioned fins can impede convection by breaking up macroscopical flows. The number, spacing, and relative positioning of fins significantly affect melting performance, and findings indicate that fin placement and geometry outperform simply raising the source temperature.

Beyond PCM systems, fins serve as additional cooling mechanisms in solar energy applications, enhancing photovoltaic efficiency. They are widely used in passive air cooling of photovoltaic (PV) panels and function as a subcategory of passive thermal management in Building Integrated Concentrating Photovoltaic (BICPV) systems; when integrated into PCM-based systems, they enhance heat dissipation from the PV panel, with longer fins providing greater cooling. Fins are also essential components in the circuitry of battery thermal management systems, including the thermal management circuits of electric vehicle batteries, where they ensure optimal temperature regulation and system stability.<SEP>Heat-transfer structures embedded in paraffin alongside metal foams to enhance heat transfer during melting.<SEP>Physical structures used to enhance the effective convective heat transfer coefficient, increasing cooling capacity at the cost of added mass and volume.<SEP>A passive heat transfer enhancement component combined with nanoparticles to improve the discharging rate of phase-change material."}
