{"entity": "Phase Change Materials", "type": "artifact", "description": "\"Phase Change Materials\" (PCMs) refers to two distinct classes of materials that share the same name: (1) thermal energy storage materials used in thermal management engineering, and (2) chalcogenide semiconductors used in electronic, photonic, and data storage applications.\n\nIn thermal engineering, Phase Change Materials are substances with a high latent heat of fusion that melt and solidify within a narrow temperature range while storing and releasing substantial amounts of heat. Examples include paraffin waxes, whose melting behavior and phase change characteristics underpin their thermal energy storage capability. These materials are employed for passive thermal management of batteries in electric vehicles, regulating battery thermal behavior and extending heat holding capacity. Implementation approaches include PCM encapsulation pouches, pure encapsulation designs, hybrid liquid cooling configurations, and combinations with heat pipe technology for hybrid thermal management of lithium-ion batteries, with passive approaches studied in contrast to active and hybrid cooling methods. PCMs are also used as passive cooling solutions for photovoltaic systems, absorbing heat and providing temperature drops without requiring energy input, and they find broader application in solar PV cooling, battery management, and green buildings.\n\nIn materials science and electronics, Phase Change Materials are a special class of semiconductor materials, most commonly composed of chalcogenide alloys such as GST-124 and GST-225, that can be reversibly switched between amorphous and crystalline phases. These thermally activated transitions are typically induced by laser pulses or controlled thermal pulses. Their exceptional optical and electrical contrast between phases makes them valuable for encoding and rewriting information in data storage, memory devices, and computing applications, as well as in photonics and nanophotonics—including optical switching, the subject of dedicated theses and reports—where their optical properties can be locally modified. PCMs undergo crystallization involving nucleation and crystal growth, and are sub-classified as nucleation-dominated or growth-dominated based on their dominant crystallization mechanism. Crystallization kinetics, cyclability, and the asymmetric crystallization window are central to their switching dynamics. Metavalent bonding plays a central role in this class, and telluride-based compounds exhibit structures amenable to fast phase transitions. These materials are studied using first-principles methods, through which new ternary compositions are predicted from binary parent compounds; additionally, confining heat within the material enhances thermal efficiency and reduces the energy required for phase transition.\n\n\"Phase Change Materials\" is also the title of a review article by Simone Raoux, published as a book by Springer US in 2009, and cited in the literature (e.g., as reference 28 in source documents)."}
