{"entity1": "Phase Change Material", "entity2": "Thermal Energy Storage", "description": "The relation (Phase Change Material, Thermal Energy Storage) denotes the relationship by which phase change materials (PCMs) are used and applied as media and mechanisms for thermal energy storage (TES). PCMs demonstrate superior thermal energy storage capability in latent heat form, storing and releasing thermal energy through melting and solidification processes and operating via charging and discharging cycles. They enable storage via latent heat within a narrow temperature range, allowing them to store substantially larger amounts of heat than sensible heat storage; beyond this primary latent heat contribution, PCMs also supply a sensible heat component that adds to the total storage capacity of a system. By absorbing heat during melting and releasing it during solidification, PCMs can extend operational hours of systems, such as distillation processes, beyond daylight periods.\n\nThis relationship spans a wide range of applications. Thermal energy storage systems utilize PCMs for purposes such as air pre-cooling, thermal management in latent heat systems, and energy storage in buildings and solar applications. Within construction, PCM integration imparts thermal energy storage capability to concrete structures, and PCMs are incorporated into mortars—including lightweight aggregate mortars and PCM–mortar composites—in which the PCM contributes latent heat to the composite-level storage capacity. PCMs are integrated into building materials, construction materials, buildings, and smart building envelopes, with PCM technology applied to building walls serving as a thermal energy storage strategy. Their energy storage function provides scientific justification for their use in sustainable construction and heritage building renovation. Enhanced additives are employed to improve thermal conductivity, and specific formulations, such as a PCM exhibiting a latent heat response near 