{"entity1": "Phase Change Material (PCM)", "entity2": "Thermal Energy Storage", "description": "The relation between Phase Change Material (PCM) and Thermal Energy Storage describes the functional role of PCMs in enabling, providing, and implementing thermal energy storage across various engineering applications. At its core, this relationship is grounded in latent heat mechanisms: PCMs provide latent-heat thermal energy storage by absorbing heat as they undergo a phase change—for example, during midday—and gradually releasing that stored heat in the afternoon and evening. This capability allows PCM systems to achieve simultaneous thermal energy storage and the distribution of heat against the temperature gradient.\n\nIn the construction sector, this relation manifests through the incorporation of PCMs into building materials. Phase change materials enable thermal energy storage in mortars and other building components, while in concrete applications, PCM functions through thermal energy storage technology specifically to improve the durability of concrete in cold climates. Research in this area has also identified a need for future work on advancing thermal modeling techniques for thermal energy storage mortars that incorporate PCM.\n\nA further significant domain of this relation is solar distillation. Phase Change Materials are regarded as a type of thermal energy storage system used to improve solar distillation efficiency. Within PCM-integrated distillation systems, the PCM provides thermal energy storage by storing heat and subsequently releasing it, thereby functioning as a thermal energy storage mechanism for the storage and release of heat during solar distillation operation.\n\nOverall, the relation between Phase Change Material and Thermal Energy Storage encompasses both the fundamental latent-heat storage capability of PCMs and its diverse practical applications, spanning building materials, cold-climate concrete durability, and solar distillation systems.<SEP>PCMs are employed in thermal energy storage systems whose heat transfer performance is studied."}
