ness</td><td>Non-corrosive</td><td>Non-corrosive</td><td>Corrosive</td><td>Non-corrosive</td></tr><tr><td>Thermal cycling stability</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Good</td></tr><tr><td>Compatibility with containers</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Limited</td><td>Good</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>Low–Moderate</td><td>Low–Moderate</td><td>Low</td><td>Moderate</td></tr><tr><td>Suitability for electronics cooling</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Limited</td><td>Moderate</td></tr></table>\nFootnotes: None\n\nAnalysis: Table 1 is a five-column, sixteen-row comparative property matrix embedded in Section 2.1 (Selection of PCM) to justify the choice of phase change material for the PCM-based heat sink investigation. Column 1 lists the evaluated property; Columns 2-5 profile Organic PCM OM 42, Organic PCM OM 55, Salt Hydrate PCM (CaCl2·6H2O, Na2SO4·10H2O), and Fatty Acid PCM (capric acid, lauric acid). Rows span quantitative thermo-physical metrics (melting temperature range, peak melting temperature, latent heat of fusion, solid/liquid density, solid/liquid thermal conductivity, specific heat capacity, volume expansion) and qualitative deployment criteria (phase segregation, supercooling tendency, corrosiveness, thermal cycling stability, container compatibility, cost, suitability for electronics cooling). Key data points: OM 42 melts at 41-43°C (peak 42°C) and OM 55 at 54-56°C (peak 55°C); both Rubitherm paraffins bracket the 40-60°C regulation band and sit comfortably below the 70°C safe electronics operating limit stated in the text. Their remaining properties are nearly identical: latent heat 190-220 vs 200-230 kJ/kg, solid density ~860 vs ~870 kg/m³, liquid density ~770 kg/m³, solid thermal conductivity 0.20-0.25 W/m·K (liquid 0.15-0.18), specific heat ~2.0-2.1 kJ/kg·K, and volume expansion 8-12%. The dominant pattern is an inverse relationship between thermal performance and practical viability. Salt hydrates lead every quantitative metric - latent heat 200-280 kJ/kg (~22% higher upper bound than OM 55), solid thermal conductivity 0.5-1.0 W/m·K (2-5x the paraffins), and solid density 1400-1600 kg/m³ (~1.6-1.9x), implying roughly double the volumetric energy storage (~360 vs ~180 MJ/m³ at mid-range values) - yet they fail every qualitative criterion: high phase segregation, moderate-high supercooling, corrosiveness, moderate cycling stability, limited container compatibility, and only 'Limited' electronics suitability. The paraffins mirror this exactly: modest thermal numbers but none/none/non-corrosive and excellent ratings for cycling stability, compatibility, and electronics suitability. Fatty acids occupy an intermediate position (latent heat 180-210 kJ/kg, low supercooling, good stability, moderate cost and suitability), consistent with the text's note that higher cost and material-compatibility issues restrict large-scale use. A secondary trade-off is that paraffins exhibit the highest volume expansion (8-12% vs 5-10% for salt hydrates and 6-10% for fatty acids), a container-sizing concern offset by their excellent container compatibility. Relationship to context: the table is the evidentiary backbone of the paper's material-selection argument, directly substantiating the surrounding statement that 'although salt hydrates provide higher latent heat and thermal conductivity, their practical application is limited by phase segregation, supercooling, and corrosiveness,' and validating OM 42/OM 55 for 'reliable, safe, and repeatable thermal regulation under varying heat loads.' The 13°C separation between peak melting points (42°C vs 55°C) directly enables the methodology's multiple-PCM (cascaded) design optimization, while the matched latent heat, density, and conductivity of the two paraffins isolate melting temperature as the controlled variable across experiments and the ANSYS 3D models of Heat Sink A (60 x 60 x 60 mm) and Heat Sink B (60 x 60 x 120 mm), supporting the 5