{"reference_id": "7", "content": "Table Analysis:\nImage Path: F:\\____IL_AI\\PCM_RAG\\lightrag\\data\\mineru_output\\Enhancing_PV_Panel_PCM-01-07_e013b54f\\Enhancing_PV_Panel_PCM-01-07\\hybrid_auto\\images\\5ca71cdf1ca989d8b6de8979b312484d475c7fbb5b9b17c8a892d02f9b0f8188.jpg\nCaption: Table 2, Thermo-Physical Properties of PCMs [21,22]\nStructure: <table><tr><td>Parameter</td><td>PCM1</td><td>PCM2</td><td>PCM3</td></tr><tr><td>Melting temperature [K]</td><td>330</td><td>334</td><td>338</td></tr><tr><td>Solidus density [kg/m3]</td><td>841.32</td><td>852.14</td><td>860.77</td></tr><tr><td>Liquids density[kg/m3]</td><td>757.25</td><td>766.11</td><td>770.53</td></tr><tr><td>Specific heat [J/kg K]</td><td>2891</td><td>2900</td><td>2952</td></tr><tr><td>Thermal conductivity [W/m K]</td><td>0.00031</td><td>0.000305</td><td>0.0003</td></tr></table>\nFootnotes: None\n\nAnalysis: Table 2 presents the thermo-physical properties of three Phase Change Materials (PCMs) labeled PCM1, PCM2, and PCM3, which correspond to RT-57, RT-61, and RT-65 based on the surrounding context. The table is organized with five thermo-physical parameters as rows (Melting temperature, Solidus density, Liquid density, Specific heat, Thermal conductivity) and three PCM types as columns. Key findings include: (1) Melting temperatures increase progressively from PCM1 (330 K / 57°C) to PCM2 (334 K / 61°C) to PCM3 (338 K / 65°C), confirming the RT-series nomenclature; (2) Both solidus density (841.32, 852.14, 860.77 kg/m³) and liquid density (757.25, 766.11, 770.53 kg/m³) increase monotonically from PCM1 to PCM3; (3) Specific heat capacity rises from 2891 J/kg·K (PCM1) to 2900 J/kg·K (PCM2) to 2952 J/kg·K (PCM3), indicating PCM3 has the highest thermal energy storage capacity per unit mass; (4) Conversely, thermal conductivity decreases from 0.00031 W/m·K (PCM1) to 0.000305 W/m·K (PCM2) to 0.0003 W/m·K (PCM3). The density change between solid and liquid phases is consistent across PCMs at approximately 10% reduction (84.07, 86.03, 90.24 kg/m³ respectively). This data is critical to the study because these PCMs are experimentally tested at constant thickness on the back surface of 250 W PV panels to evaluate cooling performance at four tilt angles (10°, 20°, 30°, 40°). The trade-off between PCM3's superior heat storage capacity (higher specific heat and density) and its inferior heat transfer characteristics (lowest thermal conductivity and highest melting point) will determine which PCM provides optimal PV panel cooling. Higher melting temperature PCMs require greater temperature rise before phase transition begins, potentially limiting their cooling effectiveness for PV panels operating in moderate temperature conditions, while their greater specific heat allows more sensible heat absorption."}
