{"reference_id": "8", "content": "Chart Content Analysis:\nContent: \n\nAnalysis: Figure 13 is a multi-panel comparative line chart (subplots a, b, c) presenting the transient surface temperature (T1) of heat sink A over a one-hour (60-minute) test period, comparing two phase change materials (PCMs) with different melting temperatures: OM42 (melting point 42°C) and OM55 (melting point 55°C). Each subplot corresponds to a distinct heater power rating: (a) 5W, (b) 10W, and (c) 15W, with time on the horizontal axis and heat sink surface temperature on the vertical axis. Each panel contains two temperature-vs-time curves, one per PCM. The chart visually supports Section 3.2.1 (Effect of PCM melting temperature) of the PCM heat sink study. Key observable behaviors: (1) At 5W, both OM42 and OM55 exhibit temperature stabilization with essentially no temperature rise from initial values, indicating effective cooling at low power; (2) At 10W, OM42 shows approximately 9.09% temperature increase versus roughly 6.89% for OM55; (3) At 15W, OM42 rises about 20% while OM55 rises only about 10.34%. The curves demonstrate that OM42's thermal regulation plateau occurs at a lower temperature because it begins melting and absorbing latent heat earlier than OM55, but OM42 consequently has a shorter thermal regulation period. OM55 sustains regulation longer and slightly reduces heat sink temperature at 10W, making OM42 suitable only when charging time is less than ~50 minutes at 15W. The figure directly follows the PCM-filled vs. PCM-free comparison (Fig. 12) and precedes the PCM thickness investigation (Fig. 14), forming a parametric study of melting temperature effects on thermal management of electronics."}
{"reference_id": "2", "content": "Ref_Text Content Analysis:\nContent: {'type': 'ref_text', 'text': '[40] K. Pielichowska, K. Pielichowski, Phase change materials for thermal energy storage, Prog. Mater. Sci. 65 (2014) 67–123.', 'bbox': [67, 558, 465, 578], 'page_idx': 20, '_content_list_index': 249}\n\nAnalysis: This is reference entry [40] from the references section of a comprehensive review paper on nano-enhanced phase change materials (NePCMs). The cited work is a foundational review article by Pielichowska and Pielichowski published in Progress in Materials Science (Volume 65, 2014, pages 67-123), titled 'Phase change materials for thermal energy storage.' As a highly cited review in a prestigious materials science journal (Impact Factor >30), this reference serves as a cornerstone source for general PCM theory, classification, and applications. Within the broader context, this reference likely supports multiple claims throughout the paper regarding PCM fundamentals, thermal energy storage mechanisms, material properties, and baseline performance characteristics against which nano-enhanced improvements are measured. The citation provides the foundational knowledge framework upon which the NePCM advancements discussed in the paper are built, establishing the baseline limitations (low thermal conductivity, phase separation, supercooling) that nanotechnology integration aims to overcome. The reference's prominence as citation [40] suggests it is one of numerous literature sources used to construct the comprehensive review of PCM technology evolution."}
{"reference_id": "4", "content": "Chart Content Analysis:\nContent: \n\nAnalysis: Figure 3 presents a temperature profile graph depicting the discharging process of Phase Change Material (PCM) with a 1:1 (kg) ratio of PCM to fish inside a coolbox. The graph plots temperature (in °C) on the y-axis against time (in minutes) on the x-axis, with data recorded every 30 minutes over a 6-hour (360-minute) period. The chart visualizes the thermal behavior during the melting phase of the PCM at room temperature. According to surrounding text, this 1:1 ratio configuration exhibited a temperature range from -4.3°C to 7.1°C during the experiment. This figure is the first in a series of discharging process figures (Figures 3-6) that systematically compare different PCM-to-fish mass ratios (1:1, 2:1, 3:1, 4:1, and 0:1 control). The 1:1 ratio represents the lowest PCM proportion tested and shows a relatively rapid temperature rise compared to higher PCM ratios, demonstrating less effective thermal regulation. This chart supports the broader discussion by establishing a baseline for evaluating how increasing PCM quantities improve cooling performance and extend temperature stability duration in coolbox applications for fish preservation."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Ref_Text Content Analysis:\nContent: {'type': 'ref_text', 'text': '18. Rajamma, R.; Ball, R.J.; Tarelho, R.A.C.; Allen, G.C.; Labrincha, J.A.; Ferreira, V.M. Characterization and use of biomass fly ash in cement-based materials. J. Hazard. Mater. 2009, 172, 1049–1060. [CrossRef]', 'bbox': [55, 267, 942, 297], 'page_idx': 13, '_content_list_index': 130}\n\nAnalysis: This is reference entry number 18 from the bibliography section of a research paper on sustainable mortar incorporating biomass ash and phase change materials (PCM). The reference cites a foundational study by Rajamma et al. (2009) published in the Journal of Hazardous Materials (volume 172, pages 1049–1060), which focuses on the characterization and application of biomass fly ash in cement-based materials. This citation is strategically positioned within the paper's reference list and serves as critical supporting literature for the authors' investigation into biomass ash as a cement replacement. The referenced work likely provides essential background on the chemical, physical, and mineralogical properties of biomass fly ash, as well as its behavior when incorporated into cementitious matrices. Given the main paper's focus on combining 20% biomass ash with PCM for sustainable mortar, this reference supports the technical rationale for utilizing biomass ash as a supplementary cementitious material, potentially informing discussions on hydration kinetics, mechanical performance, durability, and environmental benefits. The inclusion of a [CrossRef] tag indicates formal DOI-based cross-referencing, ensuring bibliographic traceability and scholarly rigor. This reference connects the current research to the established body of knowledge on biomass ash utilization in construction materials."}
{"reference_id": "5", "content": "Chart Content Analysis:\nContent: \n\nAnalysis: This chart is Figure 13, presenting the third replicate Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC) thermogram of a composite material containing 50% phase change material (PCM) and 50% clinoptilolite (a natural zeolite). As a DSC thermogram, it plots heat flow (typically in W/g) versus temperature (°C), displaying characteristic endothermic peaks during PCM melting and exothermic peaks during crystallization/solidification, from which phase transition temperatures and enthalpies are derived. The figure belongs to a series of DSC results (Figures 8-18) reporting replicate analyses performed on separate, newly prepared composite samples. Notably, the surrounding context states these tests were performed on composites that had been previously rejected due to their insufficient ability to bind PCM, indicating this replicate serves to verify or re-evaluate the thermal storage performance of the 50/50 PCM-clinoptilolite formulation. The 'third replicate' designation is significant for assessing measurement reproducibility, consistency of melting/solidification enthalpies, and the reliability of the composite's thermal energy storage capacity. This figure directly supports the paper's broader investigation into enhancing the phase transition enthalpy of shape-stabilized PCM composites using zeolite carriers, providing quantitative evidence for comparing the clinoptilolite-based composite against other carrier materials and against neat PCM performance."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Ref_Text Content Analysis:\nContent: {'type': 'ref_text', 'text': '31. Cabeza, L.F.; Miró, L.; Oró, E.; Barreneche, C. Review of thermal energy storage with phase change materials (PCMs) in buildings. Renew. Sustain. Energy Rev. 2015, 43, 531–544. [CrossRef]', 'bbox': [55, 692, 942, 722], 'page_idx': 13, '_content_list_index': 143}\n\nAnalysis: This content is reference entry #31 from the References section of a research paper published in an MDPI journal. The citation follows standard academic referencing format, documenting a review paper by Cabeza, L.F.; Miró, L.; Oró, E.; and Barreneche, C. titled 'Review of thermal energy storage with phase change materials (PCMs) in buildings,' published in Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews (Vol. 43, 2015, pp. 531-544). The reference includes a CrossRef identifier for digital access. This citation is strategically positioned within the reference list to support the paper's investigation of PCM-enhanced sustainable mortar. The cited review provides foundational knowledge on thermal energy storage applications of PCMs in building contexts, directly relevant to the current study's aim of improving thermal performance through 20% PCM addition. The work by Cabeza et al. (2015) serves as key literature establishing the scientific basis for PCM integration in construction materials, lending credibility to the experimental approach and situating the research within established scholarly discourse on building energy efficiency solutions. The bbox coordinates [55, 692, 942, 722] indicate standard reference list formatting on page 13 of the document."}
{"reference_id": "9", "content": "Table Analysis:\nImage Path: F:\\____IL_AI\\PCM_RAG\\lightrag\\data\\mineru_output\\Thermal_Barriers_Additive-01-06_d3e464db\\Thermal_Barriers_Additive-01-06\\hybrid_auto\\images\\c7398194cb49a9f2916da0ad874fbc24eb62315ccd4fdc70a8163f6637038a25.jpg\nCaption: Table 2. Comparative overview of experimental studies on different PCM oil types, including thermophysical ranges and application domains.\nStructure: <table><tr><td>PCM Type/Oil</td><td>PCM Form</td><td>Tm Range [°C]</td><td>ΔH Range [kJ/kg]</td><td>Investigated Parameters</td><td>Experimental Methods</td><td>Application Domain</td><td>Key Findings Relevant to Dynamic Behavior</td><td>Reference</td></tr><tr><td>Commercial paraffin blends (n-alkanes)</td><td>Pure PCM</td><td>5–35</td><td>150–220</td><td>Cp(T), k(T), ΔH(T), transition width</td><td>DSC, temperature-resolved property analysis</td><td>Building TES</td><td>Broader molecular distributions lead to wider transition intervals and smoother q(T) curvature.</td><td>Kraiem et al. [22]</td></tr></table>\nFootnotes: None\n\nAnalysis: Table 2 is structured as a nine-column comparative literature matrix designed to enable systematic cross-study comparison of experimental research on phase change material (PCM) oils. The columns are: 'PCM Type/Oil', 'PCM Form', 'Tm Range [°C]', 'ΔH Range [kJ/kg]', 'Investigated Parameters', 'Experimental Methods', 'Application Domain', 'Key Findings Relevant to Dynamic Behavior', and 'Reference'. This organization allows readers to correlate material thermophysical properties (melting temperature, latent heat) with experimental characterization approaches and observed dynamic thermal behavior.\n\nThe visible data row documents the study by Kraiem et al. [22] on commercial paraffin blends (n-alkanes) in pure PCM form. The reported melting temperature range of 5–35°C spans exactly the band relevant to building comfort and passive thermal management, making paraffins suitable candidates for the thermal partitions discussed in the surrounding text. The latent heat of fusion range of 150–220 kJ/kg represents substantial energy storage density, with the upper value (220 kJ/kg) approaching the theoretical maximum for practical paraffinic PCMs.\n\nThe investigated parameters—Cp(T), k(T), ΔH(T), and transition width—were measured via DSC (differential scanning calorimetry) and temperature-resolved property analysis, establishing a methodological template for characterizing temperature-dependent thermal behavior. The application domain is Building TES (thermal energy storage), which directly connects to the paper's stated goal of reducing electricity consumption and increasing building energy efficiency through PCM-containing partitions.\n\nThe key finding—that broader molecular distributions in commercial paraffin blends lead to wider transition intervals and smoother q(T) curvature—is central to the surrounding text's argument. The context explicitly states that different PCM types 'can alter the effective activation temperature and modify the heat transfer dynamics observed in q(t) and q(T), even when the nominal Tm is similar.' The Kraiem et al. row provides concrete evidence for this claim: the smeared phase transition caused by mixed n-alkane chain lengths means the PCM does not activate at a single sharp temperature but gradually across the 5–35°C interval. This has direct implications for the paper's own experimental design, which measures transient response (∆T) and steady-state parameters (λ, R) in Kelvin cell and honeycomb structures.\n\nFurthermore, this finding cautions that nominal Tm values reported in datasheets may not predict actual thermal regulation performance in printed cellular partitions—a critical consideration for the paper's proposed design framework, which must balance PCM selection (Tm, ΔH, cycle stability), carrier material properties, and geometry simultaneously. The table, spanning applications beyond additive manufacturing (unlike Table 1), broadens the evidence base for understanding PCM dynamic behavior across contexts."}
