{"reference_id": "12", "content": "Chart Content Analysis:\nContent: \n\nAnalysis: This chart is a DSC (Differential Scanning Calorimetry) thermogram panel belonging to Figure 12, captioned 'Slurry-microencapsulated PCMs,' plotting heat flow versus temperature to characterize melting (endothermic) and crystallization (exothermic) transitions. It compares individual slurry-microencapsulated PCMs with their 1:1 mixture, corresponding to the proportions used in the multi-PCM lime mortars. Key thermal parameters (cross-referenced with Table 5): MS5 melts at approximately 6°C (enthalpy 143.6 J/g) and crystallizes at -3°C (148.3 J/g); MS25 melts at ~25°C (137.5 J/g) and crystallizes at ~21°C (141.1 J/g); MS18 melts at ~18°C (160.9 J/g) and exhibits a characteristic double crystallization peak at ~14°C and ~12°C (total 159.6 J/g), attributed to subcooling effects and multiple crystallization pathways in the paraffinic system. The central finding: the 1:1 mixtures (MS5-25 or MS18-25) display two distinct phase change events with peak positions essentially unchanged relative to the individual PCMs, demonstrating that each PCM retains its characteristic thermal behavior and the system responds as the sum of independent contributions. This contrasts sharply with the form-stable system (Figure 12a), where the FS-5/25 mixture collapsed into a single broadened transition spanning 0-18°C due to interactions between non-confined paraffinic phases in the silica matrix. The independence observed here arises from polymer-shell microencapsulation, which physically isolates each paraffinic core and prevents direct contact between PCM domains. Within the broader study, this chart supports the conclusion that microencapsulation (in slurry or powder form) provides a predictable route for designing multi-PCM mortars with discrete, controllable thermal activation profiles spanning an extended temperature range."}
{"reference_id": "13", "content": "to a 15.4% increase in power output [42].\n• Hotspot mitigation and mismatch losses: In PV thermal management, 3D conductive\nskeletons play a vital role in eliminating localized hotspots. Maintaining temperature\nhomogeneity across the solar array is fundamentally important to minimize electri-\ncal mismatch losses, which can severely degrade the overall power output of the\nsystem [43].\n• The thermal-mass trade-off: While 3D metal frameworks significantly enhance heat\ndissipation, their introduction adds considerable parasitic weight to the system. This\nthermal-mass trade-off, influenced by the porosity and pore size of the metal foam,\nmust be carefully balanced against the load-bearing capacity of the PV mounting\nstructures [44].\n3.3. Advanced Shape-Stabilized PCMs (SSPCMs) and Inclinational Leakage Prevention\nUnlike horizontally flat systems, solar panels are almost exclusively installed at in-\nclined angles in outdoor environments. This architectural necessity makes the prevention of\nliquid leakage during the phase transition the most critical design challenge for PCM-based\nPV cooling.\nAdvanced shape-stabilized PCMs (SSPCMs) address this issue by encapsulating the\nphase-change components within polymer, melamine foam, or multi-structured graphite\nmatrices [45–47]. Remarkably, even when the active PCM reaches its fully molten state,\nthe composite retains its macroscopic solid form, entirely eliminating the risk of leakage,\nstructural contamination, or system failure on inclined PV surfaces.\nEnclosing PCMs within micrometer-scale shells (e.g., GO/SiO 2 hybrid shells or\ncrosslinked polystyrene) further increases the specific surface area and heat exchange\nefficiency [48,49]. Recent advancements in microencapsulated PCMs (e.g., using melamine/\nacrylic shells) not only reduced PV temperatures by 9.5 ◦C and boosted efficiency by 11.2%,\nbut also robustly restricted leakage rates to less than 1% [50].\nFor utility-scale solar farms, macro-encapsulation using 3D-printed structures, spheri-\ncal macrocapsules, or robust metallic containers provides higher cost-effectiveness, ease\nof installation, and mechanical durability [51,52]. Notably, modern macro-encapsulated\npolymeric PCM matrices have achieved a 13.2 ◦C temperature reduction with a 14.8%\nhttps://doi.org/10.3390/pr14121912\nProcesses 2026, 14, 1912 6 of 13\nefficiency boost, while demonstrating a negligible leakage rate (<1%) even under high\ninclination angles [53]. To synthesize these material-level advancements, Table 2 provides a\ncritical comparative analysis of the fundamental advantages, limitations, and commercial\nviability of different PCM architectures.\nTable 1. Performance comparison of advanced PCM composites for PV cooling.\nPCM Category Enhancement\nMaterial\nStudy Type &\nConfiguration\nOperating\nConditions\nTemp.\nReduction (◦C)\nEfficiency\nBoost (%) Citations\nNano-Enhanced Graphene Experimental\n(Indoor)\n1000 W/m2,\nambient 25 ◦C 10.04 12.15% [30]\nHybrid\nGraphene-CNT\nExperimental\n(Outdoor)\nPeak summer\nirradiance 11 14.3% [31]\nPCM\nNano-emulsions\nExperimental\n(Indoor)\n1000 W/m2,\nactive flow 18 19.5% [32]\n3D Framework Copper Foam Experimental\n(Outdoor)\nNatural\nconvection 14.5 16.2% [41]\nExpanded\nGraphite (EG)\nExperimental\n(Indoor) 1000 W/m2 12.8 15.4% [42]\nShape-Stabilized Melamine/Acrylic\nShell Numerical & Exp. 800 W/m2,\ninclined setup 9.5 11.2% [50]\nPolymeric Matrix Numerical\nOptimization\nTransient thermal\nload 13.2 14.8% [53]\nTable 2. Critical comparative analysis of different PCM architectures for PV thermal management.\nPCM Architecture\nThermal\nConductivity\n(W/mK)\nLeakage Risk Relative Cost Key Advantages Practical Limitations\nPure Organic\nPCMs Low (<0.3) High Low High latent heat\ncapacity\nThermal saturation,\nsevere leakage.\nNano-Enhanced\nPCMs Moderate (0.5–2.0) Moderate High Fast thermal response Nanoparticle\nagglomeration.\n3D Frameworks\n(Metal Foam) 