{"entity": "Thermal Inertia", "type": "concept", "description": "Thermal inertia is a thermal property of materials describing their capacity to resist, delay, flatten, and dampen temperature variations; higher thermal inertia slows temperature changes and produces stronger damping of fluctuations. It reflects a material's ability to buffer temperature swings by absorbing and releasing heat at exposed surfaces. In building physics it is quantified by effusivity, while in mortars it is determined by specific heat capacity, thermal conductivity, and density, and is closely tied to the material's heat-storage capability, with thermal storage capacity per kilogram serving as a key evaluation parameter.\n\nThe behavior of thermal inertia is demonstrated, for example, by an ambient temperature curve lagging slightly behind solar irradiation—maintaining a maximum plateau from 13h to 15h—and by the temperature lag between the top surface of a mortar specimen (subject to convective heat transfer with air) and its insulated bottom surface, where greater inertia flattens, delays, and stabilizes the bottom-surface response. Experimentally, it is assessed through heating–cooling and transient thermocouple tests, calculated from temperature-difference data between the exposed top and insulated bottom surfaces; mortars with higher inertia exhibit more pronounced differences between top and bottom surface temperatures. Equations (2)–(8) of the heating–cooling tests enable fair comparison of this property between reference mortars and PCM-impregnated mortars, while Equation (1) converts the differential thermal behavior of multi-PCM mortars into a single quantitative descriptor.\n\nThermal inertia is a critical factor in designing materials for specific thermal performance and a desirable building property, particularly important for building envelope design, as it reduces energy consumption and stabilizes indoor climates. It is significantly enhanced through the incorporation of phase change materials (PCMs), which are hosted in mortar pore networks and act as isothermal energy reservoirs via latent heat storage and release. This PCM-driven enhancement—evidenced by attenuated temperature peaks, elevated minimum temperatures, smoother and more gradual heating and cooling slopes, and a sustained thermal response that enhances thermal regulation relative to PCM-free reference mortars—depends on the volume of PCM incorporated. Among tested materials, paraffin P21 in lightweight aggregate mortar (LWAM) provided the greatest gain, quantified as a 42.92% increase in specific heat, with improvements accompanied by increased effusivity and specific heat capacity while flexural and compressive strength are preserved.\n\nA key consideration in PCM composites is the trade-off between thermal inertia and heat conduction: although PCM incorporation increases conductivity, the added thermal inertia can compensate for this penalty. The most desirable composites are those in which the thermal-inertia gain exceeds the conductivity increase, as the benefits generally outweigh the added conductivity."}
{"entity": "Phase Change Materials", "type": "artifact", "description": "\"Phase Change Materials\" (PCMs) refers to two distinct classes of materials that share the same name: (1) thermal energy storage materials used in thermal management engineering, and (2) chalcogenide semiconductors used in electronic, photonic, and data storage applications.\n\nIn thermal engineering, Phase Change Materials are substances with a high latent heat of fusion that melt and solidify within a narrow temperature range while storing and releasing substantial amounts of heat. Examples include paraffin waxes, whose melting behavior and phase change characteristics underpin their thermal energy storage capability. These materials are employed for passive thermal management of batteries in electric vehicles, regulating battery thermal behavior and extending heat holding capacity. Implementation approaches include PCM encapsulation pouches, pure encapsulation designs, hybrid liquid cooling configurations, and combinations with heat pipe technology for hybrid thermal management of lithium-ion batteries, with passive approaches studied in contrast to active and hybrid cooling methods. PCMs are also used as passive cooling solutions for photovoltaic systems, absorbing heat and providing temperature drops without requiring energy input, and they find broader application in solar PV cooling, battery management, and green buildings.\n\nIn materials science and electronics, Phase Change Materials are a special class of semiconductor materials, most commonly composed of chalcogenide alloys such as GST-124 and GST-225, that can be reversibly switched between amorphous and crystalline phases. These thermally activated transitions are typically induced by laser pulses or controlled thermal pulses. Their exceptional optical and electrical contrast between phases makes them valuable for encoding and rewriting information in data storage, memory devices, and computing applications, as well as in photonics and nanophotonics—including optical switching, the subject of dedicated theses and reports—where their optical properties can be locally modified. PCMs undergo crystallization involving nucleation and crystal growth, and are sub-classified as nucleation-dominated or growth-dominated based on their dominant crystallization mechanism. Crystallization kinetics, cyclability, and the asymmetric crystallization window are central to their switching dynamics. Metavalent bonding plays a central role in this class, and telluride-based compounds exhibit structures amenable to fast phase transitions. These materials are studied using first-principles methods, through which new ternary compositions are predicted from binary parent compounds; additionally, confining heat within the material enhances thermal efficiency and reduces the energy required for phase transition.\n\n\"Phase Change Materials\" is also the title of a review article by Simone Raoux, published as a book by Springer US in 2009, and cited in the literature (e.g., as reference 28 in source documents)."}
