{"entity": "Thermal Energy Storage", "type": "concept", "description": "Thermal Energy Storage (TES) is a technology, process, and overarching research field dedicated to capturing and storing thermal energy—often from intermittent, renewable, or waste heat sources—by heating or cooling a storage medium so the energy can be retrieved later. It is conventionally divided into three principal classes: sensible, latent, and thermochemical storage. Recognized as an important research topic for addressing increasing energy dependence and the evolving renewable energy landscape, TES offers a lower capital cost than electrochemical storage systems, typically $70–$200 per kilowatt-hour, and its value and performance can be mathematically quantified and tracked through specific equations.\n\nCentral to TES, particularly latent heat storage, are Phase Change Materials (PCMs), which absorb and release thermal energy during phase transitions. PCMs encompass organic, inorganic, paraffin- or petroleum wax-derived, biomass-based, binary eutectic, and metal- or alloy-based formulations, including Metal-Organic Framework (MOF)-based PCMs; thermochemical and sorption materials are also actively employed. Heat transfer limitations are mitigated through metal foams, wire mesh conductive enhancement, and nanotechnology: nano-enhanced PCMs (NePCMs) and nanocomposites incorporating CuO nanoparticles, carbon nanostructures, or hybrid nanomaterials significantly improve thermal conductivity, while micro-, nano-, and macro-encapsulation techniques stabilize the media. Heat transfer enhancement is a key investigative focus, spanning finned enclosures, hybrid heat sink designs combining PCM storage with conventional cooling, and techniques addressed by Ho et al. (2021); charging and discharging performance of storage media is likewise studied. PCM melting dynamics and heat transfer have been examined through numerical and experimental studies, including research on fin spacing optimization and energetic overlap in molten zones, alongside composite TES panels for latent heat storage investigated by Sari et al. and Cong et al. PCM-based TES systems also serve as passive solutions for reducing peak heat loads and stabilizing indoor temperatures.\n\nTES finds broad application in buildings and construction, where microencapsulated paraffinic and biobased PCMs are integrated into gypsum, concrete, and cementitious building envelopes to increase structural thermal capacity, provide passive storage, and regulate indoor temperature; paraffins and fatty acid-based composites similarly serve building and solar energy applications. Further applications include industry, Concentrated Solar Power (CSP) systems, solar energy storage, building thermal management, and electronics cooling—the latter evidenced by the widespread use of OM42 and OM55 PCMs owing to their stable thermophysical properties and reliable phase change behavior—as well as micro-cold storage units, with PCMs serving both heat-storage and cold-storage roles. Specialized cross-disciplinary uses, such as crown-ether-decorated phase-change microcapsules for selective ion capture, are also being explored.\n\nOverall, Thermal Energy Storage constitutes an active interdisciplinary research landscape bridging thermal engineering, materials science, nanotechnology, and energy storage systems, extensively reviewed in the literature—exemplified by works such as \"Phase change material-based thermal energy storage\"—closely associated with venues like the Journal of Energy Storage, and championed by leading researchers including L. F. Cabeza and F. Bruno."}
