{"entity": "Buildings", "type": "content", "description": "\"Buildings\" identifies two distinct entities: an academic journal and physical structures.\n\nAs an academic journal, Buildings is an open-access, peer-reviewed publication of MDPI specializing in building science, engineering, and related fields. Its publication record demonstrates a strong focus on phase change materials: it published a 2023 review article discussing the application areas of latent heat phase change materials in Volume 13, Issue 7 (article number 1595), followed by another article on phase change materials in 2024, alongside a cited paper in Volume 14 (article number 493). In 2023, it also published a thermophysical characterization of paraffins versus temperature for thermal energy storage, as well as a study on envelope airtightness in Egyptian residential dwellings. Beyond energy-related topics, the journal published \"Energy Efficiency of Historic Buildings\" in 2022 (Volume 12, article number 200) and \"Bridge Cable Performance Warning Method Based on Temperature and Displacement Monitoring Data\" in 2025 (Volume 15, article number 2342). An additional cited article appeared in 2025 (Volume 15, article number 2733), with citations following MDPI journal formatting conventions; notably, the journal uses article numbers rather than page ranges.\n\nIn a separate context, \"Buildings\" denotes physical structures, specifically within the residential and industrial sectors. These structures account for a major portion of global energy expenses and represent a significant application sector for energy storage and phase change materials. Buildings are subjected to diurnal cycles, seasonal variations, and climate-dependent fluctuations that generate continuously shifting thermal profiles. Over 20% of European buildings were constructed before 1945 and often exhibit poor thermal performance and elevated energy consumption. Buildings also serve as the application domain for passive and active PCM systems targeted at thermal management and energy conservation, as the domain in which thermal simulation of phase change materials is demonstrated by the cited article by Hongxia Zhou, Åke Fransson, and Thomas Olofsson, and as a modern field where thermal switches are applied for evaluating energy efficiency. To enhance energy efficiency and store thermal energy, various phase change materials—such as nano-enhanced phase change materials and thermally enhanced mortars—are integrated into core structural components, including walls, roofs, and floors, and these material applications are specifically utilized within such physical structures to effectively mitigate overheating.<SEP>Academic journal that published the 2025 performance study by Alrasheedi and colleagues on nano-enhanced PCM in building-integrated semi-transparent photovoltaic modules."}
