{"entity": "Compressive Strength", "type": "concept", "description": "Compressive strength is a mechanical property of cementitious materials—including pastes, mortars, concrete, hardened cementitious composites, and grouts—that reflects their capacity to withstand axial loading until failure. It is typically evaluated in dedicated testing phases following established standards; for example, ASTM C349-18 was applied to 72 mortar samples using the 8 × 4 × 4 cm halves obtained from flexural strength tests, and it serves to assess mechanical performance after specific curing and drying protocols.\n\nCompressive strength is frequently discussed in relation to Phase Change Materials (PCMs). In most cases, incorporating PCMs reduces compressive strength—typically by 5% to 30%—because PCMs weaken the microstructure and hinder cement hydration. Excessive PCM content can cause even greater losses, with reductions of up to 50% observed with 15 wt.% PCM microgranules and about 45% with octadecane. In one study of pastes at 28 days of hydration, the highest value was recorded for CB10 (54.21 MPa) and the lowest for CB20_20 (9.30 MPa), with PCM causing a significant reduction. Similarly, partially replacing cement with biomass ash reduces the compressive strength of cement–lime pastes, with higher ash contents producing moderate reductions. However, exceptions exist: in mortars, PCM addition increased compressive strength by up to 15% in lightweight aggregate mortar, while improvements in NAM and RCAM mortars were less significant. In multi-PCM formulations, compressive strength is generally maintained, being highest for form-stable systems (up to ca. 3 MPa) and lowest for powder microencapsulated mortars (ca. 1 MPa). Compressive strength in mortars is also inversely proportional to aggregate porosity, as demonstrated in reported experimental results.\n\nMetakaolin acts as a key enhancer of compressive strength: its incorporation increases this property in mortars, provides moderate improvements in multi-PCM systems, and influences strength development in high-strength concrete. In lime-metakaolin grouts, compressive strength was evaluated in a 2018 study on superplasticizers. Compressive strength also serves to evaluate the load-bearing capacity of mortars, and all studied formulations satisfied the requirements for lime-based rendering mortars. Reported compressive strength measurements (Figure 8) correlate with freeze-thaw durability results (Figure 9), with higher compressive strength contributing to freeze-thaw resistance and linking mortar microstructure to durability.\n\nFinally, certain processing and design strategies preserve or optimize compressive strength: the vacuum incorporation method does not compromise this property in hardened cementitious composites, and the effusivity-enhancement method maintains it while improving the composite's thermal effusivity. Although PCMs can negatively impact compressive strength, this property can be optimized through the proper selection of PCM materials."}
