{"entity1": "PCM", "entity2": "Thermal Inertia", "description": "The relation (PCM, Thermal Inertia) describes the effect of Phase Change Material (PCM) incorporation on the thermal inertia of mortars. Across all descriptions, the consistent finding is that PCM incorporation—whether mixed into or impregnated within mortar—increases, enhances, or augments the thermal inertia of the resulting material, a conclusion regarded as central to the research from which these descriptions derive.\n\nBeyond the fundamental increase in thermal inertia, PCM incorporation transforms mortars into thermally active materials. This enhanced thermal performance manifests as temperature stabilization: temperature variations within the mortar become slower and delayed, and fluctuations are damped. Notably, the magnitude of this effect depends on the quantity of PCM present, with a higher absorbed PCM mass producing slower temperature variations.\n\nThe descriptions also address the trade-offs and conditions associated with this relation. Adding PCM to mortar imposes a conductivity penalty—a reduction in thermal conductivity—yet the gains in thermal inertia compensate for this drawback. More specifically, PCM incorporation is considered beneficial when improvements in specific heat and effusivity exceed the conductivity penalties incurred. Importantly, these thermal benefits are achieved without compromising the mechanical integrity of the mortars, meaning the material retains its structural performance while acquiring enhanced thermal properties.<SEP>The incorporated PCM volume determines the thermal inertia and phase-change stabilization capacity of the PCM-enhanced mortars.<SEP>PCM incorporation increases the thermal inertia of the mortars, attenuating temperature peaks and producing smoother heating and cooling responses."}
