{"reference_id": "2", "content": "Impact of the phase change material (PCM) integration on the mortar’s thermal inertia: The role of the aggregates and PCM types\n\nMatias Alvarez-Rodriguez <sup>a,b,\\*</sup> , Marina Lopez-Arias <sup>b</sup> , Mirian Velay-Lizancos <sup>b</sup> , Mar Alonso-Martinez <sup>a</sup>\n\n<sup>a</sup> University of Oviedo, GICONSIME Research Group, C/Pedro Puig Adam s/n, Gijon, ´ Asturias 33204, Spain\n\n<sup>b</sup> Lyles School of Civil and Construction Engineering, Purdue University, West Lafayette, IN 47907, USA\n\nA R T I C L E I N F O\n\nKeywords: Mortar Phase change material Thermal inertia Lightweight aggregate Recycled concrete aggregate Cementitious composites Thermal energy storage\n\nA B S T R A C T\n\nIntegrating phase change materials (PCMs) into building materials has gained attention for enhancing thermal performance and energy efficiency. However, the impact of different PCM types on cementitious materials thermal and mechanical properties requires further study. This work addresses this gap by incorporating two biobased esters and a paraffin-based PCM into natural aggregate mortar (NAM), recycled concrete aggregate mortar (RCAM), and lightweight aggregate mortar (LWAM). Thermal and mechanical properties were evaluated using the transient plane source method, heating and cooling cyclic tests, and standard mechanical tests. PCM impregnation was influenced by both PCM viscosity and mortar porosity. High-viscosity PCMs (paraffin) achieved greater absorption in more porous mortars like LWAM, while the incorporation of low-viscosity PCMs (esters) was higher in less porous matrices. LWAM absorbed the highest PCM mass (12.55 g), while NAM and RCAM had higher percentages of filled pores (up to 23.85 % and 28.92 %, respectively). PCM incorporation improved thermal inertia across all mortars, but there was a tradeoff between the rise in conductivity and thermal inertia. LWAM demonstrated the best overall performance, with the lowest thermal conductivity (1.04–1.15 W/m⋅K) and highest specific heat (921.92–1243.10 J/kg⋅K). PCM addition also increased the compressive and flexural strength of LWAM by up to 15 %, with less significant improvements observed in NAM and RCAM. This study’s novelty is its fundamental understanding of the combined effects of PCM viscosity, mortar porosity, and PCM type on thermal and mechanical properties, contributing to the deployment of energy efficient construction materials.\n\n1. Introduction\n\nReducing greenhouse gas emissions is one of society’s primary goals to minimize the effects of global warming. The International Energy Agency stated that 34 % of final energy consumption is due to the buildings sector, contributing to 37 % of total $\\mathrm { C O } _ { 2 }$ emissions worldwide [1], and 40 % of the energy consumption of buildings comes from heating and cooling [2]. Under the current trend, the average surface temperature is expected to rise by $2 . 7 ~ ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C }$ in 2100 [3]. In consequence, governments and organizations proposed strategies to reduce energy consumption like the Green Deal [4], the European Climate Law [5], and the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development [6].\n\nLatent heat thermal energy storage reduces energy demand, improves the efficiency of HVAC systems, and tackles the mismatch between energy demand and supply [7]. This can be achieved with the incorporation of phase change materials (PCMs) in buildings [8]. PCMs store and release latent thermal energy when they incur in a phase change process, which might be a modification of the state of aggregation (solid-liquid, liquid-gas, solid-gas) or an internal molecular structure rearrangement (solid-solid or liquid-liquid) [7]. Solid-liquid PCMs are preferred for building applications because their latent energy is higher than same-phase PCMs, while the volume variation between the phases is reduced [9]. Thus, PCMs store thermal energy when they are heated and melt, and they release the stored energy when they are cooled and solidify. While PCMs undergo the phase change process, their temperature is almost constant, creating isothermal energy reservoirs, increasing the thermal inertia of building envelopes, reducing energy consumption, and improving thermal comfort inside of the building [10].\n\nPCMs are categorized according to their chemical composition into organic (paraffins and non-paraffins), inorganic (salt hydrates, metals, and alloys), and eutectic mixtures [11]. The desired properties of PCMs for building envelope applications are high latent heat and specific heat capacity, reduced volume changes, non-corrosiveness, non-toxicity, non-flammability, chemical stability, absence of subcooling, and a reasonable price and availability on the market [12]. Although no PCM fulfills all these requirements, organic PCMs are the fittest for con struction applications [13].\n\nThe incorporation of PCMs into cementitious materials recently gained attention since they might be used to manufacture active PCM walls, floors, and roofs [14]. However, the phenomenological interactions between PCMs and cement are still not fully understood, and more research is needed [15].\n\nThe simplest method is directly incorporating the PCM while mixing cement products, but it is not an effective method due to leakage, chemical reactions, or microstructure modification [16]. Other tech niques were proposed to overcome this, such as shape-stabilization and microencapsulation.\n\nShape-stabilization consists of containing the PCM in the pores of a supporting material, which is then added to cementitious materials. The supporting materials comprise clay-based powders like diatomite [17] or montmorillonite [18], carbon-based materials like expanded graphene [19] or biochar [20"}
{"reference_id": "2", "content": "Table Analysis:\nImage Path: F:\\____IL_AI\\PCM_RAG\\lightrag\\data\\mineru_output\\PCM_Mortars_Thermal_Inertia-01-07_af3763c8\\PCM_Mortars_Thermal_Inertia-01-07\\hybrid_auto\\images\\514dbbee0b8537c14d49b3f8ebc4f6f104df6a655c71194652d247a3d5bf3a68.jpg\nCaption: Table 1, Material quantities for $1 ~ \\mathrm { m } ^ { 3 }$ of mixture.\nStructure: <table><tr><td>Mixture</td><td>Cement (kg)</td><td>Fine sand (kg)</td><td>NA (kg)</td><td>RA (kg)</td><td>LWA (kg)</td><td>Effective Water (kg)</td></tr><tr><td>NAM</td><td>732.47</td><td>920.90</td><td>335.34</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.00</td><td>292.99</td></tr><tr><td>RCAM</td><td>732.47</td><td>920.90</td><td>0.00</td><td>280.61</td><td>0.00</td><td>292.99</td></tr><tr><td>LWAM</td><td>732.47</td><td>920.90</td><td>0.00</td><td>0.00</td><td>247.60</td><td>292.99</td></tr></table>\nFootnotes: None\n\nAnalysis: Table 1 presents the mix design (material quantities per 1 m³ of mixture) for the three mortars investigated in the study: natural aggregate mortar (NAM), recycled concrete aggregate mortar (RCAM), and lightweight aggregate mortar (LWAM). The table has seven columns: mixture identifier, cement (kg), fine sand (kg), and three mutually exclusive columns for the 0–4.75 mm aggregate types — NA (natural silica aggregate), RA (recycled concrete aggregate), and LWA (lightweight expanded clay, ARLITA® S) — plus effective water (kg). The zeros (0.00) in the unused aggregate columns make explicit that each mortar contains exactly one coarse-fraction aggregate type. Three key patterns emerge: (1) Cement (732.47 kg, CEM II/A-V 42.5 R), fine sand 0–2 mm (920.90 kg), and effective water (292.99 kg) are identical across all three mixtures, confirming the stated 0.4 water-to-cement ratio exactly (292.99/732.47 = 0.400) and the equal volumetric proportion of the 0–4.75 mm aggregate. (2) The only variable is aggregate mass: 335.34 kg (NA) vs. 280.61 kg (RA) vs. 247.60 kg (LWA). Because the aggregate volume is held constant, the mass differences directly reflect decreasing particle density: RA is ~16.3% lighter than NA (due to adhered porous cement mortar on recycled concrete), and LWA is ~26.2% lighter than NA and ~11.8% lighter than RA (due to the porous core of expanded clay granules). (3) Resulting total mixture masses decline from 2281.70 kg (NAM) to 2226.97 kg (RCAM) to 2193.96 kg (LWAM), implying progressively lower mortar density. This table is methodologically central to the surrounding content: by fixing every variable except fine aggregate type, it isolates aggregate porosity as the sole factor governing subsequent vacuum impregnation with the three PCMs (one paraffin, two biobased esters). It operationalizes the study's novelty — analyzing the interaction between mortar porosity and PCM viscosity — and connects to the literature review, where lightweight/porous aggregates (expanded shale, slate, perlite, pumice) served as PCM carriers (Esmaeeli, Aguayo, Mohseni) and recycled aggregates were used for sustainability (Suttaphadkee, Rostami). The 'effective water' definition (excluding sorption by dry materials) and oven-drying of aggregates ensure comparability despite the very different water-absorption capacities of NA, RA, and LWA."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "a dynamic thermal response that depends on the material’s temperature and on the absorbed mass of PCM (which depends on the mortar type and PCM).\n\nThese observations are essential for designing materials tailored to specific thermal performance requirements, where the magnitude of thermal inertia and temperature stabilization are critical factors.\n\n4. Conclusions and future work\n\nThis study incorporated three types of phase change materials (PCMs) into different mortars with varying porosities to investigate their impact on thermal, mechanical, and physical properties. A paraffin PCM and two ester PCMs were used in three types of mortars: natural aggregate mortar (NAM), recycled concrete aggregate mortar (RCAM), and lightweight aggregate mortar (LWAM). The following conclusion were drawn based on the experimental results:\n\n• Using a paraffin PCM with a 21 ◦C melting point and its impregnation in LWAM offered the best solution among all studied in terms of thermal properties. It showed the highest increase in specific heat capacity (42.92 %), maintaining a low conductivity (1.08 W/m⋅K).\n\n• PCM incorporation in mortars leads to a tradeoff between the in crease of the thermal conductivity and the improvement of the thermal inertia. In most cases, the rise in effusivity and specific heat outweighed the increase in conductivity, which is beneficial for reducing energy consumption in buildings. Overall, LWAM showed the best performance since it has the lowest conductivity and highest specific heat capacity after PCM incorporation. However, certain combinations of mortar-PCM might be especially interesting depending on the targeted specific heat and maximum admissible conductivity.\n\n• The interaction between mortar porosity and PCM viscosity plays a decisive role in the incorporated mass of PCM. PCMs with low viscosity are better suited for mortars with lower porosity. In contrast, more viscous PCMs lead to greater incorporation for mortars with a higher porosity.\n\n• PCM addition has a negligible impact on the mechanical properties of NAM and RCAM. In contrast, the mechanical performance of LWAM was improved, indicating that PCM incorporation slightly enhances the strength of porous mortars.\n\n• The incorporation of PCM into mortars altered the transient thermal response. Mortars with PCM exhibit delayed temperature variations due to latent heat exchange during phase transitions. The specific combination of mortar type and PCM led to different thermal responses, indicating that the particular PCM must be carefully chosen depending on the application.\n\nThe experimental findings prove that PCM incorporation into mortars improves thermal performance while maintaining structural integrity, particularly in high-porosity materials like LWAM. This work provides a fundamental understanding of the mechanisms involved in PCM impregnation in cement-based composites. The analysis of the interaction between the aggregate and the PCM type contributes to the development of the incorporation of PCMs into precast elements, such as concrete blocks, which are widely used in construction. This approach ensures controlled impregnation while maintaining structural integrity and optimizing thermal efficiency, allowing for the design of thermal efficient buildings targeted for specific climates.\n\nTo contribute to the deployment of this technology in building en velopes, the following future works are needed:\n\n• Broadening the present work with other PCMs, mortar mixture formulations (altering, for example, the water-to-cement ratio), and incorporation times.\n\n• Measuring the energy storage capacity and the dynamic thermal response of PCM-incorporated mortars applying temperature curves to actual size samples.\n\n• Evaluating the long-term performance of PCM-impregnated mortars under repeated thermal cycling conditions to ensure their durability and stability. Such studies are essential to complement the current findings and validate the practical applicability.\n\n• Advancing the thermal modeling techniques for thermal energy storage mortars. This is especially relevant to study the thermal performance of building envelopes or even whole building systems that incorporate these materials.\n\n• Performing the life cycle and economic analysis of mortars with PCM to evaluate their practical application in sustainable construction.\n\nCRediT authorship contribution statement\n\nLopez-Arias Marina: Writing – review & editing, Visualization, Validation, Software, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation. Alvarez-Rodriguez Matias: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Visualization, Validation, Software, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation. Alonso-Martinez Mar: Writing – review & editing, Supervision, Resources, Project administration, Methodology, Funding acquisition, Formal analysis, Conceptualization. Velay-Liz ancos Mirian: Writing – review & editing, Visualization, Validation, Supervision, Resources, Project administration, Methodology, Funding acquisition, Formal analysis, Conceptualization.\n\nDeclaration of Competing Interest\n\nThe authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper.\n\nAcknowledgments\n\nThis work was supported by the Spain Ministry of Universities (FPU21/05062 and EST24/00362) and the Spain Ministry of Science and Innovation through Research Projects PID2021-128056OA-I00 and"}
{"reference_id": "6", "content": "et al., Computer modelling and experimental investigation of phase \nchange hysteresis of PCMs: The state-of-the-art review, Appl. Energy 263 (2020) \n114572, https://doi.org/10.1016/J.APENERGY.2020.114572 .\n[56] M.H. Zahir, S.A. Mohamed, R. Saidur, F.A. Al-Sulaiman, Supercooling of phase- \nchange materials and the techniques used to mitigate the phenomenon, Appl. \nEnergy 240 (2019) 793 – 817, https://doi.org/10.1016/J.APENERGY.2019.02.045 .\n[57] L. Liu, X. Zhang, X. Xu, Y. Zhao, S. Zhang, The research progress on phase change \nhysteresis affecting the thermal characteristics of PCMs: a review, J. Mol. Liq. 317 \n(2020) 113760, https://doi.org/10.1016/J.MOLLIQ.2020.113760 .\n[58] K.P. Verian, W. Ashraf, Y. Cao, Properties of recycled concrete aggregate and their \ninfluence in new concrete production, Resour. Conserv Recycl 133 (2018) 30 – 49, \nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/J.RESCONREC.2018.02.005 .\n[59] J. Xiao, W. Li, Z. Sun, D.A. Lange, S.P. Shah, Properties of interfacial transition \nzones in recycled aggregate concrete tested by nanoindentation, Cem. Concr. \nCompos 37 (1) (2013) 276 – 292, https://doi.org/10.1016/J. \nCEMCONCOMP.2013.01.006 .\n[60] D.C. Montgomery. Design and Analysis of Experiments, 8th ed., John Wiley & Sons \nInc, New York, 2012 .\nM. Alvarez-Rodriguez et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Construction and Building Materials 470 (2025) 140642 \n15"}
{"reference_id": "4", "content": "expand the availability of \nPCM options suitable for concrete applications, \n• developing partnerships between PCM manufacturers and concrete \nproducers to ensure compatibility and streamline integration \nprocesses, and \n• investing in research and development efforts to optimize PCM \nformulations specifically for concrete applications, addressing \ncompatibility challenges. \nERDC/CRREL TR-23-11 14 \n \n3 Conclusions and Recommendations \n3.1 Conclusions \nBy optimizing PCM selection, utilizing appropriate additives, considering \nlong-term durability, addressing cost concerns, and enhancing availability \nand compatibility, PCM-infused concrete can be effectively implemented \nwhile maintaining or even improving its overall performance. Studies have \nreported a reduction in compressive strength ranging from 5% to 30% when \nincorporating PCMs into concrete, depending on factors such as PCM type, \ncontent, and distribution within the matrix. The presence of PCMs can lead \nto a decrease in workability, with a slump loss of approximately 10% to 30% \nreported in some cases. Setting time can be extended by 1 to 4 hours \nbecause of the interference of PCMs with the cement hydration process. The \naddition of PCMs can result in a slight reduction in concrete density, \ntypically ranging from 1% to 5%. PCMs may increase the porosity of \nconcrete, resulting in an increase of 1% to 8% compared to conventional \nconcrete. PCM-infused concrete may exhibit some degradation due to \nfactors such as increased permeability and potential chemical interactions \nwith the PCM. The extent of the impact on long-term durability varies based \non the specific PCM used, but it can affect properties such as freeze-thaw \nresistance and resistance to chemical attack. PCMs are generally more \nexpensive than conventional concrete materials on a per-unit basis. The cost \nof PCMs can range from $5 to $50 per kilogram, depending on factors such \nas PCM type, purity, and production scale. While there is a growing range of \nPCMs available on the market, the selection of suitable PCMs specifically \ndesigned for concrete applications is still relatively limited. Compatibility \nissues may arise during the manufacturing and integration of PCMs into \nconcrete, requiring adjustments to mixing procedures or the use of \nspecialized techniques. \nIt is important to note that the specific quantitative impacts can vary based \non the PCM type, concentration, and specific application context. \nMitigation strategies, such as optimizing PCM selection, using additives, \nand employing specific manufacturing techniques, can help minimize \nthese challenges and maximize the benefits of PCM-infused concrete. \nERDC/CRREL TR-23-11 15 \n \n3.2 Recommendations \nOur recommendations are to include testing PCMs in concrete \nconstruction applications based on function, cost, and constructability for \nthe efficacy of PCMs in concrete mixtures, and effect the of PCMs on other \nconcrete properties."}
{"reference_id": "3", "content": "Article\n\nBiomass Ash and Phase Change Material (PCM) for Energy Efficiency of Sustainable Cement–Lime Composites\n\nCynthia Guardia <sup>1</sup>, Ana Guerrero <sup>2</sup> and Gonzalo Barluenga <sup>1,</sup>\\*\n\n1 Department of Architecture, Universidad of Alcalá, 28801 Madrid, Spain; cynthia.guardia@uah.es\n\nInstitute of Construction Science Eduardo Torroja, CSIC, c/Serrano Galvache 4, 28033 Madrid, Spain; aguerrero@ietcc.csic.es\n\nCorrespondence: gonzalo.barluenga@uah.es\n\nAbstract\n\nSustainability and nearly zero-energy consumption of new and existing buildings is a keystone in the new guidelines established by the European Commission. Likewise, waste management is in the focus of reducing the impact of industrial processes. The use of industrial byproducts, such as biomass ashes (BA), can be an interesting solution for waste valorization, reducing the carbon footprint and enhancing sustainability. In addition, Phase Change Materials (PCMs) can be used for improving energy efficiency due to their thermal storage capacity. An experimental study on the effect of biomass ash (BA) and PCM on the microstructure, chemical, physical and mechanical properties of cement–lime pastes was carried out. The partial replacement of cement with BA reduced compressive strength although did not substantially modify other paste properties, while the addition of PCM had a huge impact on microstructure and, therefore, physical and mechanical properties. PCM had a remarkable effect on thermal properties, endowing thermal storage capacity and reducing thermal conductivity, and the combination with BA further improved paste thermal properties.\n\nKeywords: biomass ash; phase change materials; cement–lime; microstructure; mechanical properties; thermal properties\n\nCheck for updates\n\nAcademic Editor: Xiaohu Yang\n\nReceived: 30 December 2025 Revised: 26 January 2026 Accepted: 27 January 2026 Published: 29 January 2026\n\nCopyright: © 2026 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license.\n\n1. Introduction\n\nBuildings are currently required to comply with increasingly demanding international regulations aimed to improving sustainability and energy efficiency, in response to reduced CO emissions [1–3]. Regarding construction and building materials, efforts head towards developing sustainable materials with reduced carbon footprint and energy and resources consumption [4–7]. The valorization of industrial byproducts and waste management through their incorporation into cement-based materials (CBM) is a promising strategy to reduce the environmental footprint of construction and building materials, promoting circular economy and new production cycles [8–10]. Recycled materials are a good ex ample [11–14]. The savings in their manufacturing (efficiency and sustainability) and the improvements in the mechanical, physical and thermal properties that these materials can provide, make recycled materials a good option [15–17].\n\nAmong these byproducts, ashes have been, for decades, investigated as supplementary cementitious materials (SCM) due to their availability, chemical composition and potential pozzolanic and filler effects [18–23]. In particular, biomass ashes (BA), generated as a residue of biomass combustion for energy production, represent a resource whose management poses increasing environmental challenges. Biomass ashes are characterized by low organic content, irregular shape, rough surface, low density and porosity, and lower water absorption compared to conventional siliceous aggregates and are used as supplementary cementitious materials in construction and building materials [24–28]. Biomass ashes can partially replace cement without significantly compromising the physical and mechanical properties of the materials. Cement substitution by biomass ash leads to a direct reduction in $\\mathrm { C O } _ { 2 }$ emissions associated with clinker production, which is responsible for substantial global emissions. Therefore, the use of BA in cementitious systems not only mitigates environmental impacts but also provides an effective pathway for waste valorization and resource efficiency in the construction sector. The incorporation of BA to CBM does not significantly modify their physical and mechanical properties while helping to reduce $\\mathrm { C O } _ { 2 }$ emissions and improving the carbon footprint [18–29].\n\nDesigning energy-efficient materials is another approach towards sustainability in buildings, reducing energy consumption by taking advantage of passive energy systems and limiting the use of active systems like air conditioning or heating [29–41]. In this regard, phase change materials (PCMs) have emerged as advanced thermal energy storage solutions to mitigate energy consumption of buildings. A wide range of PCMs has been developed, including organic, inorganic and hydrated salts [30–32], which can be incorporated as microencapsulated additives for CBM [36–40]. PCMs change phase (from liquid to solid and vice versa) at a specific temperature (melting point) [40], storing and releasing thermal energy while maintaining a constant temperature (latent heat). Among them, organic phase change materials have been widely adopted in building applications due to their high latent heat, chemical stability and absence of phase segregation. Microencapsulated PCM paraffin can be incorporated into cement-based matrices, preventing leakage during phase change and preserving the integrity and durability of the composite material. Previous studies have demonstrated that microencapsulated PCMs can significantly improve the thermal storage capacity of construction and building materials [42–48].\n\nAccordingly, the aim of this study is to enhance both sustainability and energy efficiency of cement-based pastes through the combined incorporation of biomass ashes as a partial cement replacement and microencapsulated PCM as a thermal energy storage additive (Figure 1). Biomass ash is used to reduce carbon footprint associated with cement production, whereas PCM incorporation aims to improve the thermal performance of the material by increasing its latent heat storage capacity.\n\nA complete chemical, mineralogical, physical, mechanical and thermal characterization was"}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Chart Content Analysis:\nContent: \n\nAnalysis: This chart appears as a figure on the final page (page index 5) of a research paper on phase change material (PCM)-incorporated mortars, positioned immediately after the Conclusions, CRediT authorship statement, and Acknowledgments sections. The figure has a wide, horizontal aspect ratio (bounding box 134,66 to 495,226), typical of a time-series or comparative plot. Given the paper's core findings, this chart most plausibly visualizes the transient thermal response experiments central to the study: temperature-versus-time curves comparing mortars with and without PCM incorporation. The conclusions explicitly state that 'the incorporation of PCM into mortars altered the transient thermal response' and that 'mortars with PCM exhibit delayed temperature variations due to latent heat exchange during phase transitions,' making a thermal inertia demonstration figure the strongest contextual match. Such a chart would likely display heating/cooling curves for the three mortar types studied—natural aggregate mortar (NAM), recycled concrete aggregate mortar (RCAM), and lightweight aggregate mortar (LWAM)—potentially with and without the three PCMs (paraffin PCM with 21°C melting point, and two ester PCMs). Key expected elements include: temperature stabilization/plateau regions near the PCM melting point (~21°C) during phase transitions, delayed temperature peaks relative to reference (PCM-free) mortars, and differential responses depending on the specific mortar-PCM combination. The chart serves as visual evidence supporting the paper's central conclusion that PCM impregnation improves thermal inertia and temperature buffering in cement-based composites, particularly in high-porosity materials like LWAM, which is fundamental to the proposed application of PCM-impregnated precast elements (e.g., concrete blocks) for energy-efficient building envelopes. No caption or footnote was extracted with the figure, limiting definitive identification, but its placement and dimensions are consistent with a summary experimental results figure (e.g., temperature evolution under thermal cycling) supporting the thermal inertia conclusions."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Mathematical Equation Analysis:\nEquation: $$\n\\widehat {e} _ {r e f} = - 1 1 6 9. 4 5 \\cdot k ^ {2} + 4 1 8 9. 3 6 \\cdot k - 1 9 8 1. 0 9\\tag{12}\n$$\nFormat: latex\n\nMathematical Analysis: Equation (12) is an empirical second-order polynomial regression model, $ē_{ref} = -1169.45k^2 + 4189.36k - 1981.09$, obtained by fitting experimental data from reference (PCM-free) mortar samples via the least-squares method. It predicts the expected thermal effusivity $ē_{ref}$ of a reference mortar as a function of its thermal conductivity $k$. The circumflex (hat) notation denotes a predicted/estimated value, distinguishing the model output from measured effusivity. Thermally, effusivity is defined as $e = \\sqrt{k \\cdot \\rho \\cdot C_p}$ (units W·s½/(m²·K)), quantifying a material's ability to exchange heat with its surroundings during transient contact—a key parameter for thermal inertia in building envelope applications. Because effusivity depends on the square root of the product of conductivity, density, and specific heat, a purely theoretical dependence on $k$ alone would scale as $\\sqrt{k}$ only if $\\rho$ and $C_p$ were constant; the quadratic form empirically captures the co-variation of density and specific heat with conductivity across mortars of differing porosity (NAM, RCAM, LWAM). Mathematically, the parabola opens downward (negative leading coefficient $a = -1169.45$), with a vertex (maximum) at $k = -b/(2a) ≈ 1.79$ W/(m·K) and real roots near $k ≈ 0.56$ and $k ≈ 3.02$ W/(m·K), so the model is physically meaningful and monotonically increasing over the conductivity range of the studied mortars. Within the paper's framework, Eq. (12) serves as the reference baseline curve (dotted line in Fig. 11); curves parallel to it delimit a gray scatter band containing all reference-sample results. This band enables a rigorous assessment of PCM-incorporated mortars: if a PCM mortar's effusivity rises above the band while its conductivity increase stays proportionally smaller (i.e., $Δe(‰) > Δk(‰)$, the 'green region' criterion in Table 7), the added thermal inertia compensates for the conductivity penalty. Together with the analogous specific heat model of Eq. (11), it operationalizes the central tradeoff analysis of the study, supporting the conclusion that LWAM with PCM achieves the highest specific heat at minimum conductivity."}
{"reference_id": "4", "content": "4). LCAs consider the entire life cycle of \nPCM-enhanced buildings, including raw material extraction, \nmanufacturing, construction, use phase, and end-of-life. These studies \nhave found that despite the additional energy and emissions associated \nwith PCM production and integration, the overall environmental \nbenefits in terms of energy savings and reduced operational emissions \noutweigh the impacts of PCM implementation. \nIt is worth noting that the specific findings may vary depending on \nfactors such as the type of PCM, its concentration, the climate, the \nbuilding design, and the local energy mix. However, most studies \nindicate that PCM integration has the potential to significantly reduce \nenergy consumption and emissions, contributing to more sustainable and \nenergy-efficient buildings.  \n2.2 Usage of PCMs in Concrete \nThere are various ways to incorporate PCMs in concrete, such as adding \nthem to the mix or embedding them in prefabricated panels. Different \ntypes of PCMs can be used depending on the desired thermal behavior and \napplication. For example, paraffin-based PCMs are often used for low-\ntemperature applications, while salt hydrates are preferred for high-\ntemperature applications.  \nThe potential applications of PCMs in concrete are vast. They can be used in \nvarious building components, such as walls, roofs, and floors, to improve \nthermal performance and energy efficiency. Additionally, PCMs can be used \nin both new construction and retrofit projects, making them a versatile and \nadaptable solution for sustainable building design. As research and \nERDC/CRREL TR-23-11 9 \n \ndevelopment in this field continues, there is potential for further \nadvancements in the use of PCMs in concrete and other building materials.  \n2.3 Incorporating PCMs into Concrete and the Effects on the \nThermal Properties and Performance of the Resulting Composite \nMaterial \nOne approach for incorporating PCMs into concrete is to mix the PCM \nwith the concrete mixture directly. In this method, the PCM particles are \ndispersed throughout the concrete matrix. As the temperature rises, the \nPCM melts and absorbs heat, and as the temperature drops, the PCM \nsolidifies and releases heat. However, the incorporation of PCM directly \ninto the concrete mix may result in poor dispersion of the PCM particles, \nleading to non-uniform thermal performance of the material. \nA second approach is to use prefabricated PCM modules or capsules that \nare embedded in the concrete. In this method, the PCM is encapsulated in \na container or a tube, which is then placed in the concrete during casting. \nThis approach allows for better control over the location and distribution \nof the PCM, but it may increase the complexity and cost of the \nconstruction process. \nA third approach is to use PCM-containing admixtures, which are added to \nthe concrete mix to enhance its thermal performance. These admixtures \ntypically contain microencapsulated PCMs that are mixed with the \nconcrete. This method offers better control over the dispersion of PCM \nparticles, and it can also improve the workability of the concrete mixture. \nHowever, the effectiveness of the admixture may depend on the type and \namount of PCM used, as well as the curing conditions of the concrete. \nThe incorporation of PCMs into concrete has several benefits, including \nincreased thermal mass, reduced temp erature fluctuations, and \nimproved energy efficiency. However, the effectiveness of the PCM in \nconcrete depends on several factors, including the type and amount of \nPCM used, the method of incorporation, and the environmental \nconditions. Therefore, the sel ection of the appropriate PCM and \nincorporation method is critical to achieving optimal thermal \nperformance in concrete.  \nERDC/CRREL TR-23-11 10 \n \n2.4 Challenges of PCMs in Concrete \nWhile PCMs offer thermal benefits, their incorporation into concrete can \nintroduce challenges in other aspects.  \nThe addition of PCMs to concrete can lead to a reduction in compressive \nstrength. PCMs may hinder the cement hydration process, affecting the \nformation of cementitious crystals and resulting in lower strength \ndevelopment. The extent of strength reduction depends on factors such as \nPCM type, content, and distribution within the concrete matrix. \nPCMs can influence the workability and setting time of concrete. The \npresence of PCMs may increase the viscosity of the concrete mixture, \nmaking it less workable and more difficult to handle during construction. \nFurthermore, PCMs can extend the setting time, as they can interfere with \nthe hydration process and delay the formation of hardened cement paste. \nPCMs often have lower densities compared to typical concrete \ncomponents. Incorporating PCMs into concrete can reduce the overall \ndensity of the composite material, potentially affecting its mechanical \nproperties and weight-bearing capacity. Additionally, the introduction of \nPCMs may alter the pore structure of concrete, potentially impacting its \ndurability and resistance to freeze-thaw cycles or chemical attacks. \nThe long-term durability of concrete can be affected by the presence of \nPCMs. The interaction between PCMs and the concrete matrix may \ninfluence moisture transport properties, pore structure, and chemical \nreactions. This can result in potential challenges related to freeze-thaw \nresistance, alkali-silica reaction, or other forms of degradation. To \novercome the stability challenge, researchers and manufacturers are \nexploring several potential solutions, including developing stable PCMs, \nencapsulation of PCMs, and using additives such as nanoparticles, to \nenhance the stability of PCMs and improve their performance in concrete. \nThe use of PCMs in concrete can also present several challenges related to \ncost, which must be addressed to ensure their effective and widespread \nuse. The cost of PCMs is generally higher than that of conventional \nbuilding materials. This is because PCMs are often"}
{"reference_id": "3", "content": "Ref_Text Content Analysis:\nContent: {'type': 'ref_text', 'text': '30. Zhou, D.; Zhao, C.Y.; Tian, Y. Review on thermal energy storage with phase change materials in building applications. Appl. Energy 2019, 92, 593–605. [CrossRef]', 'bbox': [55, 662, 942, 690], 'page_idx': 13, '_content_list_index': 142}\n\nAnalysis: This is a bibliographic reference entry (#30) from the references section of a scholarly article about sustainable biomass ash-PCM mortar. The cited work is a review article by Zhou, Zhao, and Tian published in Applied Energy (Vol. 92, pp. 593–605) addressing thermal energy storage using phase change materials (PCMs) in building applications. As a review paper, this reference likely provides foundational context on PCM integration strategies, thermal performance mechanisms, and building application methodologies. Within the broader research context, this citation supports the paper's investigation of PCM-enhanced mortar by establishing theoretical background on how phase change materials contribute to thermal energy storage in construction materials. The reference is structured in standard academic citation format with author names, article title, journal name, year, volume, page range, and a CrossRef identifier for digital access. This source is particularly significant as it bridges the gap between PCM technology generally and the specific application in building materials that the current research investigates."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Chart Content Analysis:\nContent: \n\nAnalysis: Fig. 10 is a two-panel bar chart presenting the mechanical properties of the studied mortars: (a) flexural strength and (b) compressive strength. The chart compares three mortar types—NAM (natural aggregate mortar), RCAM (recycled concrete aggregate mortar), and LWAM (lightweight aggregate mortar)—both as reference cases (without PCM) and with incorporated phase change materials (PCM, including P21 and ester variants such as E19). For the reference mortars, a band indicating the amplitude of variation is displayed, serving as a benchmark: PCM-modified values falling outside these bands signify a relevant PCM effect. Average difference values in flexural and compressive strength are also annotated. The chart illustrates the key findings of Section 3.5 (Mechanical properties): (1) strength is inversely proportional to aggregate porosity—RCAM flexural/compressive strength is 13.32%/13.25% lower than NAM, while LWAM is 38.12%/51.40% lower; (2) PCM incorporation does not significantly affect flexural or compressive strength of NAM and RCAM, but produces a substantial flexural strength increase in LWAM, attributed to its highest absorbed PCM mass and initially lowest strength; (3) no PCM leakage occurred after compression testing, validating the vacuum-incorporation method. The figure underpins the paper's broader argument that PCM incorporation enhances thermal inertia without compromising mechanical integrity, supporting subsequent thermal property analysis (Fig. 11) and transient thermal response evaluation (Figs. 12–14)."}
{"reference_id": "3", "content": "Chart Content Analysis:\nContent: {'type': 'chart', 'img_path': 'F:\\\\____IL_AI\\\\PCM_RAG\\\\lightrag\\\\data\\\\mineru_output\\\\Biomass_Ash_and_Phase_Change_Material_PCM_for_Energy_Efficiency_of_Sustainable_CementLime__8ddda994\\\\Biomass_Ash_and_Phase_Change_Material_PCM_for_Energy_Efficiency_of_Sustainable_CementLime_\\\\hybrid_auto\\\\images\\\\f4de5ef5e7dd5aa82314feea70e538006234f608866a6ca5dc75a1755ecedc96.jpg', 'content': '', 'chart_caption': ['Figure 8. Effect of biomass ash and PCM content on thermal conductivity and open porosity.'], 'chart_footnote': [], 'bbox': [297, 504, 813, 747], 'page_idx': 10, '_content_list_index': 93}\n\nAnalysis: Figure 8 is a scatter plot chart illustrating the relationship between open porosity accessible to water (OP) on the x-axis and thermal conductivity values on the y-axis, distinguishing between two PCM states: solid state (λS) and liquid state (λL). The chart plots data points from various paste mixtures with differing PCM and biomass ash (BA) replacement levels, organized to reveal how porosity and thermal conductivity interact. Key findings include: (1) λL was consistently lower than λS across all mixtures due to the lower thermal conductivity of liquid microencapsulated PCM; (2) PCM addition at 10% and 20% replacement levels reduced both OP and thermal conductivity, with higher PCM content producing lower values for both parameters; (3) an inverse relationship between OP and thermal conductivity is visible within each PCM content group, but when grouped solely by PCM content, the relationship appears direct because PCM simultaneously reduces both OP and thermal conductivity; (4) higher BA content slightly increased OP and correspondingly reduced both λS and λL. The chart serves as a critical bridge between the mechanical properties discussion (Section 4.2, which established similar OP correlations with compressive strength) and the thermal properties analysis (Section 4.3), demonstrating that the waterproofing shell and spherical shape of PCM microcapsules govern the porous network, which in turn controls thermal behavior. This visualization supports the broader argument that PCM microcapsules, rather than BA, are the primary drivers of microstructural and thermal property changes in cement-lime pastes."}
{"reference_id": "2", "content": "incorporation method, well suited for precast production elements, was proposed in a recent study [37]. In this method, the PCM is incorporated through vacuum into the pore structure of hardened cementitious composites. The results showed that this method enhances the composite’s effusivity without compromising its compressive strength.\n\nWhile the results of this recent study are very promising, this new work goes further by addressing two critical aspects that have received little attention: the role of PCM properties and the porosity of aggregates in the effectiveness of this novel incorporation method. Specifically, this study fills a gap in the literature by comparing the incorporation of different types of PCMs (one paraffin and two biobased esters) into three types of mortars, defined by their fine aggregate: natural, recycled concrete, and lightweight. The effect of the interaction between the mortar porosity and the PCM viscosity is analyzed, as it directly influences the amount of PCM that can be absorbed and the thermal performance of the mortar with PCM. The novelty of this work is analyzing how different PCM types impact the thermal and mechanical properties of mortars with various aggregates. This assessment is critical for defining a standardized procedure for PCM incorporation as a function of the composite’s properties and the behavior of the PCM used, aiming at practical implementation in both precast and broader construction applications.\n\n2. Materials and methods\n\n2.1. Materials\n\nThe analyzed mortars were manufactured with CEM II cement (CEM II/A-V 42.5 R), water, fine natural silica sand 0 – 2 mm (sand-0/2) and fine aggregate 0 – 4.75 mm. This study assessed three types of 0 – 4.75 mm aggregate (natural silica aggregate NA-0/4.75, recycled concrete aggregate (RCA-0/4.75), and lightweight aggregate (LWA-0/\n\n4.75), yielding three types of mortar: natural aggregate mortar (NAM), recycled concrete aggregate mortar (RCAM), and lightweight aggregate mortar (LWAM).\n\nThe natural aggregate in the NAM is silica sand. The recycled con crete aggregate in the RCAM was retrieved from a local quarry. The lightweight aggregate in the LWAM is expanded clay ARLITA® S [38]. It consists of granules with a porous core. The granulometry of these aggregates and the sand $- 0 / 2$ is presented in Fig. 1. The lightweight aggregate is a commercial product whose granulometry is given by the manufacturer. However, the natural and recycled concrete aggregates come from local quarries. The NA and RCA granulometry was customized to resemble the lightweight aggregate as much as possible. The differences between the sieving curves of the different aggregates are due to the disparity between European and American sieves. These differences are negligible for the scope of this work.\n\nTable 1 shows the mixing proportions of the studied mortars. In all cases, the volume proportion of the 0 – 4.75 mm aggregate was the same, and the water-to-cement ratio was 0.4. Therefore, the only difference between the mortars is the 0 – 4.75 mm aggregate type used. To design the mixtures of the different mortars, the dosage was done in volume of dry components per total volume of the mixtures. The effective water is the water added to the mixture without including the water sorption by the dry materials. The aggregates were oven-dried before the mixing process.\n\nThe mixing of the mortars followed the ASTM C305–20 standard [39]. For each mortar type, 18 samples were cast with $1 6 \\times 4 \\times 4$ cm dimensions. In total, 54 samples were manufactured. The samples were set in molds for 24 hours. The samples were covered with a plastic film to avoid water loss during the setting time in molds. After hardening, the samples were de-molded and placed into a wet chamber to cure for 28 days at $9 5 \\pm 5 \\ : \\% \\ : \\mathrm { R H }$ and $2 5 \\pm 1 ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C }$ . Once cured, the samples were dried in $\\mathrm { a ~ 6 0 ~ ^ { \\circ } C }$ oven until the weight loss between two consecutive days was lower than 0.2 %. After drying, they were stored in laboratory conditions (50 ± 5 % RH and $2 1 \\pm 1 ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C } )$\n\nThe PCMs that were incorporated into the mortars are RT21-HC (P21), CrodaTherm 21 (E21), and CrodaTherm 19 (E19). P21 is a paraffin mixture with a phase change temperature of around ${ 2 1 ~ ^ { \\circ } \\mathrm { C } }$ manufactured by Rubitherm GmbH [40]. E21 and E19 are bio-based esters derived from plant-based feedstocks. They were produced by Croda International Plc. and have phase change temperatures of around 21 ◦C [41] and 19 ◦C [42], respectively.\n\n2.2. Methods\n\nThe experimental campaign was designed to characterize the PCMs and the mortars with and without PCM. 18 samples of each mortar type were manufactured: 3 were used to determine the mortar density and permeable porosity, 3 for the pore size analysis, 3 for the thermal and mechanical characterization as reference samples without PCM, and the remaining 9 samples were used for PCM incorporation and characterization. Fig. 2 shows the scheme of the experimental campaign with the tests performed for the PCM and mortar characterization.\n\n2.2.1. PCM characterization\n\nThe viscosity and thermal properties of the studied PCMs were analyzed since they affect the samples’"}
{"reference_id": "5", "content": "Chart Content Analysis:\nContent: {'type': 'chart', 'img_path': 'F:\\\\____IL_AI\\\\PCM_RAG\\\\lightrag\\\\data\\\\mineru_output\\\\Multi-PCM_Lime_Mortars_Incorporating_Pol__p0029-0035_ef92b754\\\\Multi-PCM_Lime_Mortars_Incorporating_Pol__p0029-0035\\\\hybrid_auto\\\\images\\\\e9b605cf32ff7137bb59bf953faf6933e9b828b68b195b70d005a3e86b6e8385.jpg', 'content': '', 'chart_caption': ['b', 'a'], 'chart_footnote': [], 'bbox': [122, 181, 840, 357], 'page_idx': 33, '_content_list_index': 202}\n\nAnalysis: This chart is a two-panel figure (subpanels labeled 'a' and 'b') presenting temperature evolution profiles obtained from the hotbox dynamic thermal cycling experiments on multi-PCM lime mortars, corresponding to Figure 14 in the publication. The figure displays temperature-versus-time curves recorded under a wide thermal cycling programme between −10 and 50 °C, a range chosen to ensure complete melting and crystallization of all incorporated PCMs (covering both low- and high-transition PCM components). The panels compare the PCM-free reference mortar (LM-MK) with a representative PCM-containing formulation (FS5-25-MK), with behavior stated to be representative of all multi-PCM mortars tested. Key visual features include: (1) clear attenuation of temperature peaks during heating and troughs during cooling in the PCM-containing mortar relative to the reference; (2) a more gradual heating and cooling response, reflecting increased thermal inertia associated with latent heat storage/release. This figure serves as the qualitative/visual counterpart to the quantitative thermal response metrics summarized in Table 7, which reports peak temperature reductions of up to 1.5 °C (heating) and 1.1 °C (cooling), maximum stage-wide temperature differences of 4.7 °C during heating (MP18-25-MK) and 2.2 °C during cooling (MS18-25), and integrated energy exchange values reaching 8.2·10^5 °C·s/m² (MS18-25). The chart bridges the DSC thermal activation analyses (Figures 12 and 13) to the dynamic performance assessment, supporting the paper's conclusion that distributing phase transitions across multiple temperature intervals produces a broader, sustained thermal buffering effect suitable for building envelopes under variable climatic conditions with high diurnal or seasonal thermal contrasts."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Chart Content Analysis:\nContent: \n\nAnalysis: This chart is a figure extracted from a research article on phase change material (PCM)-incorporated mortars for building thermal inertia applications (page 6, page_idx 5). The automated content extraction returned no text, caption, or footnote for this figure (empty content string, no chart_caption or chart_footnote entries), indicating the chart consists purely of graphical elements (likely plotted curves, bars, or data visualizations) without machine-readable embedded text. Based on its position in the document and the surrounding conclusions text, this chart most plausibly visualizes experimental thermal performance data supporting the study's key findings. The paper investigates three PCMs (a paraffin PCM with 21°C melting point and two ester PCMs) impregnated into three mortar types: natural aggregate mortar (NAM), recycled concrete aggregate mortar (RCAM), and lightweight aggregate mortar (LWAM). The chart likely depicts one of the following: (1) transient temperature response curves showing delayed temperature variations in PCM mortars due to latent heat exchange during phase transitions (directly supporting the conclusion that 'mortars with PCM exhibit delayed temperature variations'), (2) specific heat capacity comparisons across mortar-PCM combinations (supporting the reported 42.92% maximum increase for paraffin PCM in LWAM), (3) thermal conductivity/effusivity tradeoff data (supporting the conductivity-vs-thermal-inertia tradeoff conclusion, with LWAM achieving 1.08 W/m·K), or (4) temperature stabilization behavior demonstrating thermal buffering effects. The figure serves as empirical evidence for the conclusions section, validating that PCM incorporation improves thermal performance while maintaining structural integrity, particularly in high-porosity LWAM. It connects to the broader discussion on designing thermally efficient building envelopes, precast elements, and climate-specific construction materials. The chart's dimensions (bbox width ~363, height ~161 points) suggest a wider-than-tall layout, consistent with time-series temperature curves or grouped comparison plots."}
{"reference_id": "5", "content": "Ref_Text Content Analysis:\nContent: {'type': 'ref_text', 'text': '68. Yuan, X.S.; Wang, B.M.; Chen, P.; Luo, T. Study on the Frost Resistance of Concrete Modified with Steel Balls Containing Phase Change Material (PCM). Materials 2021, 14, 4497. [CrossRef]', 'bbox': [55, 464, 942, 494], 'page_idx': 42, '_content_list_index': 315}\n\nAnalysis: This ref_text content is a single bibliographic reference entry (reference number 68) extracted from the references section of an academic document, located on page index 42 (bounding box coordinates [55, 464, 942, 494], content list index 315). The citation follows standard academic reference formatting and contains the following structural elements: (1) four co-authors — Yuan, X.S.; Wang, B.M.; Chen, P.; and Luo, T.; (2) the paper title 'Study on the Frost Resistance of Concrete Modified with Steel balls Containing Phase Change Material (PCM)'; (3) publication venue — the peer-reviewed journal Materials; (4) publication details — year 2021, volume 14, article number 4497; and (5) a CrossRef hyperlink designation. The scientific subject matter of the cited work concerns civil engineering and construction materials research, specifically investigating how concrete modified with encapsulated phase change materials (PCMs) — contained within steel balls — performs under frost/freeze-thaw conditions. This suggests the citing document likely deals with topics such as phase change materials in construction, concrete durability, thermal energy storage in building materials, or cold-climate infrastructure. As a reference entry, its primary function is knowledge attribution and source linkage within the parent document's citation network. Key retrieval terms include: phase change material (PCM), steel ball encapsulation, frost resistance, concrete modification, freeze-thaw durability, thermal regulation, and Materials journal (MDPI)."}
{"reference_id": "1", "content": ", whose viscosity ranges from 5.2 mPa⋅s to 6 mPa⋅s. This is observed in the almost constant slope of the response plane for low viscosity values. In the case of the paraffin, whose viscosity is 12.6 mPa⋅s, the porosity is more relevant: the higher the porosity, the greater the PCM incorporated mass.\n\nThus, in low-porosity mortars (NAM and RCAM), using highviscosity PCMs is detrimental to the incorporation. In contrast, in high-porosity mortars (LWAM), although PCM viscosity has less influence, the PCM incorporation is maximized with higher viscosity PCM. This means that porous mortars are suitable for PCMs with a large range of viscosity. Meanwhile, for low-porosity mortars, using PCMs with low viscosity is critical to optimizing the absorption of PCM. This highlights that PCM incorporation is not solely determined by viscosity or porosity alone but also by their interaction. For instance, while lightweight aggregate mortars exhibit higher porosity, the absorbed PCM volume varies depending on the PCM type, underscoring the importance of this analysis for practical applications.\n\n3.5. Mechanical properties\n\nThe average flexural strength and compressive strength of the studied mortars are presented in Fig. 10. In the cases without PCM, flexural and mechanical strength are inversely proportional to the porosity of the aggregate. Thus, the highest values are found for NAM and the lowest values for LWAM. More specifically, the flexural strength of RCAM and LWAM is 13.32 % and 38.12 % lower than in NAM, respectively. The compressive strength of RCAM was 13.25 % lower than in NAM, and in LWAM it was 51.40 % lower.\n\nFor the reference cases, a band with the amplitude of the variation was added. This helps noticing if the effect of the PCM incorporation in the mechanical properties of mortars with PCM is relevant. Values of flexural or compressive strength outside the bands of the reference mortars indicate that the effect of adding PCM is relevant. The values of the average difference in flexural strength and compressive strength are included in Fig. 10 too. PCM incorporation does not significantly affect the flexural strength of NAM and RCAM, but in LWAM, it leads to a substantial increase. This increment happens because, in LWAM, the incorporated mass of PCM was the greatest, and this type of mortar initially had the lowest flexural strength. Similar trends are observed in compressive strength results. This reveals that PCM incorporation does not compromise the mechanical properties of the studied mortars.\n\nIt is also highlighted that after the compression tests no PCM leaked the samples, demonstrating that the vacuum-incorporation method prevents this issue.\n\n3.6. Thermal properties\n\nThe incorporation of PCM into the pores of the mortar seeks to increase thermal inertia. However, PCMs have a thermal conductivity up to 10 times higher than the thermal conductivity of the air they are substituting. As such, the incorporation of PCM presents a tradeoff between the increase of the thermal inertia (effusivity and specific heat capacity) and the increase of the conductivity of the composite mortar-\n\nPCM.\n\nFig. 11 presents the mortar samples’ conductivity, effusivity, and specific heat capacity. The results show a correlation between the conductivity and the other two properties. Eq. (11) shows the expected specific heat capacity depending on the conductivity of the reference sample ${ \\widehat { C p } } _ { r e f } ,$ and Eq. (12) shows the expected effusivity $\\widehat { e } _ { r e f }$ . These equations were obtained by fitting the data of the samples without PCM to a second-order polynomial equation using the least squares method. They are represented in Fig. 11 by the dotted lines.\n\nThe gray area, built with curves parallel to Eqs. (11) and (12), includes the results of all the reference samples. Both the specific heat capacity and the effusivity increase when the conductivity rises. For the reference samples, LWAM shows the lowest conductivity, effusivity, and specific heat. This is explained by the high porosity of this type of mortar and the insulating effect of the air. In contrast, NAM presents the highest conductivity, effusivity, and specific heat. This is due to the lower porosity of this type of mortar. RCAM showed in-between values of the thermal properties since its porosity is lower than in NAM but higher than in LWAM. However, RCAM presented more dispersion in the results due to the higher heterogeneity of the aggregate.\n\nFocusing on the results of the mortars with PCMs, it is observed that they show higher conductivity, effusivity, and specific heat than the corresponding reference mortar. As shown in Fig. 11, in LWAM and NAM, the increase of specific heat capacity is higher than the increase of conductivity with all the PCMs. In RCAM, this trend is not as clear due to the higher variability of the samples. Besides, in RCAM with P21, the specific heat did not increase with the increase in conductivity. Therefore, in NAM and LWAM with PCM, the increase in thermal inertia is greater than the increase in conductivity. This situation is the desired case because it implies that, even if the PCM increases the conductivity, the augmented thermal inertia compensates for it. More specifically, LWAM is the best solution because it reaches the highest values of specific heat with minimum conductivity.\n\nThe effusivity shows a similar trend but with less significant increments. This happens because of the definition of effusivity, as it depends on the square root of the product of several variables (con ductivity, density, and specific heat).\n\nTo better understand the tradeoff between thermal inertia and con duct"}
