{"entity": "Phase-Change Memory", "type": "concept", "description": "Phase-Change Memory (PCM), also known as PRAM, PCRAM, Ovonic Unified Memory (OUM), or ovonic memory, is a non-volatile memory technology that stores data through rapid, reversible transitions of phase-change materials—primarily chalcogenide glasses—between crystalline and amorphous structural states. Its development traces to Ovshinsky's 1968 discovery of switching in chalcogenide glasses, with intellectual lineage to Yamada's 1987 work; early commercialization research involved STMicroelectronics, Politecnico di Milano, and Ovonyx, and it has since become a significant research field pursued by groups such as IBM Research's PCM team.

PCM operation exploits the large resistivity contrast between the low-resistance crystalline (SET) and high-resistance, multi-megaohm amorphous (RESET) phases. Electric pulses induce Joule heating to switch states—melt-quench amorphization (RESET) and crystallization (SET)—and data are read by sensing resistance. Threshold switching is an essential accompanying principle, since the high RESET resistance would otherwise demand hundreds of volts; it manifests as subthreshold leakage, voltage snapback, and threshold-voltage behavior, studied extensively via time-resolved electrical measurements and compact physics-based models. Key materials include Ge2Sb2Te5 (GST), GeSbTe, GeTe, AgInSbTe, and SiSb, with Ge–Sb–Te chalcogenide superlattices (interfacial PCM) offering substantial performance enhancement through structural-motif and bonding optimization.

By combining non-volatility with nanosecond access times—demonstrated in GeTe cells—PCM constitutes storage-class memory, bridging fast volatile memories (SRAM, DRAM) and slower non-volatile storage, and positioning it as a competing candidate in the "memory race." Unlike flash, PCM benefits from downscaling: cells scaled below the average nucleus size become growth-dominated, making nucleation time negligible, accelerating SET switching, and providing a viable path toward DRAM-speed non-volatile memory. The resistance contrast also enables multilevel (multi-bit) storage.

Principal challenges include resistance drift—the amorphous phase's resistance increases over time through structural relaxation—together with threshold-voltage drift, which degrades multilevel retention, read-out reliability, and read/write margins. Mitigation requires predictive drift modeling and compensation, drift-resistant material design, and write strategies ensuring reliable resistance-level separation; around 2011, reducing power consumption, programming current, and switched material volume remained major goals.

PCM has been the subject of extensive academic study, including dissertations (e.g., that of Gunnar Bruns), early-2000s scaling and reliability analyses, and electrical characterization work employing VTR/CTR diagrams, pulse-shape-aware programming, and device-physics modeling of threshold switching, crystallization kinetics, and data retention, often through academic–industry partnerships.<SEP>Memory technology application targeted by the inverse-designed resistive heaters using Sb2S3.<SEP>Memory technology that relies on reliable Sb2S3 amorphization/crystallization, serving as the application target of the inverse-designed resistive heaters.<SEP>A memory technology application domain targeted by the inverse-designed resistive heater, using phase-change materials such as Sb2S3."}
{"entity": "Chalcogenide Materials", "type": "naturalobject", "description": "Chalcogenide Materials are a class of chemical compounds containing elements from Group 16 of the periodic table—the chalcogens, notably sulfur (S), selenium (Se), and tellurium (Te)—that possess distinctive functional properties. Functioning as semiconductors, they serve as the fundamental materials basis for phase-change memory (PCM) technology and emerging non-volatile storage solutions.

The defining characteristic of chalcogenide materials is their ability to undergo reversible structural phase transitions between amorphous and crystalline states. These two phases exhibit dramatically different physical properties, including an intrinsic resistivity contrast spanning roughly six orders of magnitude and markedly different optical reflectivity. This contrast is precisely what phase-change memory devices exploit for data storage. Prototypical examples include Ge2Sb2Te5 (GST) and GeTe, along with the broader family of Ge-Sb-Te alloys, which are used in chalcogenide-based memory cells and films.

In their amorphous form, chalcogenides display several electrical behaviors central to memory operation, including threshold switching between high-resistance amorphous and conductive states, subthreshold conduction, low-field amorphous state resistance, threshold voltage drift, and stochastic amorphous-to-crystalline phase transitions. Their crystallization and amorphization dynamics, governed by both electronic and thermal mechanisms, enable the operation of phase-change random access memory.

Chalcogenide materials are the subject of extensive research spanning device physics, reliability of non-volatile memory, electrothermal and phase-change dynamics, amorphous semiconductor physics, and memristive devices. Notably, their electronic and thermal phase-transition mechanisms for memory applications were investigated in Gunnar Bruns' doctoral thesis, underscoring their continued scientific and technological significance."}
{"entity": "Phase Change Materials", "type": "artifact", "description": ""Phase Change Materials" (PCMs) refers to two distinct classes of materials that share the same name: (1) thermal energy storage materials used in thermal management engineering, and (2) chalcogenide semiconductors used in electronic, photonic, and data storage applications.

In thermal engineering, Phase Change Materials are substances with a high latent heat of fusion that melt and solidify within a narrow temperature range while storing and releasing substantial amounts of heat. Examples include paraffin waxes, whose melting behavior and phase change characteristics underpin their thermal energy storage capability. These materials are employed for passive thermal management of batteries in electric vehicles, regulating battery thermal behavior and extending heat holding capacity. Implementation approaches include PCM encapsulation pouches, pure encapsulation designs, hybrid liquid cooling configurations, and combinations with heat pipe technology for hybrid thermal management of lithium-ion batteries, with passive approaches studied in contrast to active and hybrid cooling methods. PCMs are also used as passive cooling solutions for photovoltaic systems, absorbing heat and providing temperature drops without requiring energy input, and they find broader application in solar PV cooling, battery management, and green buildings.

In materials science and electronics, Phase Change Materials are a special class of semiconductor materials, most commonly composed of chalcogenide alloys such as GST-124 and GST-225, that can be reversibly switched between amorphous and crystalline phases. These thermally activated transitions are typically induced by laser pulses or controlled thermal pulses. Their exceptional optical and electrical contrast between phases makes them valuable for encoding and rewriting information in data storage, memory devices, and computing applications, as well as in photonics and nanophotonics—including optical switching, the subject of dedicated theses and reports—where their optical properties can be locally modified. PCMs undergo crystallization involving nucleation and crystal growth, and are sub-classified as nucleation-dominated or growth-dominated based on their dominant crystallization mechanism. Crystallization kinetics, cyclability, and the asymmetric crystallization window are central to their switching dynamics. Metavalent bonding plays a central role in this class, and telluride-based compounds exhibit structures amenable to fast phase transitions. These materials are studied using first-principles methods, through which new ternary compositions are predicted from binary parent compounds; additionally, confining heat within the material enhances thermal efficiency and reduces the energy required for phase transition.

"Phase Change Materials" is also the title of a review article by Simone Raoux, published as a book by Springer US in 2009, and cited in the literature (e.g., as reference 28 in source documents)."}
