{"entity": "Electronic Memory", "type": "concept", "description": "Electronic Memory is a fully electronic memory concept based on phase-change materials, first proposed by Neale et al. in 1970. Conceived as a memory element usable in computers or comparable applications, it represents a foundational idea within the broader field of phase-change memory, where it is frequently discussed as a topic addressing memory and storage concepts.

The operating principle of Electronic Memory relies on the marked contrast in resistivity between the amorphous and crystalline states of phase-change materials. This resistance contrast forms the basis for storing information, and stored data is subsequently read out by sensing the material's resistance through the application of voltage pulses.

With respect to material selection, phase-change materials exhibiting fast growth speeds are regarded as candidate materials for electronic memories. Among these, GeTe in particular has been identified as having strong potential for implementation in Electronic Memory, especially in small, growth-dominated devices."}
