{"entity": "PCRAM", "type": "artifact", "description": "Phase-Change Random Access Memory (PCRAM), also known as phase-change memory, is a non-volatile data storage technology that encodes binary information by exploiting the enormous and persistent resistance contrast between the amorphous and crystalline phases of phase-change materials. The high-resistance amorphous phase represents the RESET state, while the low-resistance crystalline phase represents the SET state, and the device utilizes both the optical and electrical contrasts of phase-change materials to perform SET and RESET operations. The distinct temperature dependences of these two phases underpin the technology's reliable non-volatile operation.

As a memory technology application of phase-change recording materials, PCRAM is based on stable chalcogenide compounds that have been widely studied for storage. Among these, Ge-Sb-Te materials are commonly employed, with device performance depending strongly on their crystallization behavior and thermal stability. GeTe crystallization within nanoseconds has been demonstrated in mushroom-type cells, a representative device architecture. Practical operation of PCRAM also relies on threshold switching.

The technology offers fast access times and holds the potential to compete with DRAM. However, it faces a significant challenge in resistance drift. The drift equation is indispensable for PCRAM, as resistance drift predicts degradation of the resistance contrast, limits long-term data retention, and causes threshold shifts, thereby necessitating drift compensation in read-out circuitry. In PCRAM cells, logarithmic-time increases of the activation energy shift the RESET resistance level, endangering multilevel retention and read-out margins; drift prediction and compensation in this context rely on Equation 6.9. Overall, PCRAM is a memory technology enabled by the distinct properties of the amorphous and crystalline phases of phase-change materials, combining rapid switching, non-volatility, and strong programmable resistance contrast, while requiring careful management of drift phenomena for reliable long-term and multilevel operation."}
