{"entity1": "Phase-Change Materials", "entity2": "Phase-Change Memory", "description": "The relation (Phase-Change Materials, Phase-Change Memory) captures the fundamental dependence of phase-change memory on phase-change materials. Phase-change memory (PCM, also referred to as PRAM) is an emerging electronic memory concept and non-volatile storage technology that is based on phase-change materials, employing them as its functional medium to store data and information.

This relationship centers on the distinctive physical properties of phase-change materials. Phase-change memory relies on their reversible amorphous-crystalline transitions, which are fundamental to the technology's operation. More specifically, the memory exploits the crystallization and glass-formation kinetics of phase-change materials—processes governed by the Gibbs free energy—along with fast atomic rearrangements that occur within these materials.

The large resistivity (resistance) contrast between the amorphous and crystalline states of phase-change materials is what enables information storage, allowing phase-change memory to achieve access times on the order of a few nanoseconds. Through these mechanisms, phase-change materials form the basis of emerging phase-change memory technologies, and research on their crystallization behavior is directly connected to PCM applications. In sum, this relation expresses that phase-change materials serve as the enabling medium whose phase-transition behavior, kinetics, and resistance contrast make non-volatile phase-change data storage possible."}
