Analysis: Figure 5.1 is a four-panel time-resolved measurement chart ('Threshold switching delay') from a doctoral thesis on phase-change memory (PCM), presenting oscilloscope-style traces of simultaneously recorded applied voltage and current signals for four rectangular test pulses with amplitudes of 770 mV, 790 mV, 870 mV, and 980 mV. Each panel shows a rectangular voltage pulse with a steep leading edge (~1 ns rise time) applied to a GeSbTe phase-change memory cell in its amorphous RESET state, along with the corresponding current response. The key observation illustrated is that the current does not rise simultaneously with the voltage edge: instead, it initially increases only linearly with time, then after a characteristic delay time (τ_d) exhibits a steep, sudden rise marking the threshold switch from the high-resistance OFF state to the conductive ON state. The four panels are arranged to demonstrate the systematic dependence of τ_d on pulse height: at 770 mV the delay is longest, and as the applied voltage increases through 790 mV and 870 mV to 980 mV, the delay time progressively shortens until, at the highest voltage, the current rise occurs essentially simultaneously with the voltage edge. This figure serves as the foundational experimental evidence for section 5.1.1 (Rectangular Pulse Experiment), directly confirming the threshold switching delay first reported by Lavizzari and Karpov in 2008 and validating the experimental design using rectangular pulses with precisely controlled leading edges. It provides the raw time-domain data from which the delay times are extracted and plotted semilogarithmically in Figure 5.2 as a function of applied voltage and initial RESET state resistance, supporting the chapter's broader goal of characterizing the nature of threshold switching in chalcogenide phase-change materials and reconciling differing experimental observations across research groups."}
