{"entity": "Sb2S3", "type": "naturalobject", "description": "Sb2S3, also known as antimony sulfide, antimony trisulfide, or stibnite, is a chemical compound and semiconductive crystal recognized as an emerging wide-bandgap, quasi-1D (low-dimensional) semiconductor and chalcogenide phase-change material (PCM). Characterized by low defect formation energies, it is actively researched for its structure, dynamics, and defect engineering, with applications in photovoltaics and solar cells. It exists in glassy (amorphous, bandgap 2.0 eV) and crystalline (bandgap 1.7 eV) forms that display starkly different surface charging and conductivity, yielding distinctly different electrical and electromechanical behaviors depending on phase.

Sb2S3 undergoes reversible, non-volatile switching between amorphous and crystalline states, accompanied by a red-to-green color shift under optical microscopy and confirmed by Raman spectroscopy. Reported thermal parameters vary slightly across sources—melting points of 823 K versus approximately 801 K, and crystallization/glass-transition temperatures of 598 K versus approximately 573 K. Crystallization occurs between Tg and Tm, while amorphization requires melting above Tm (peak ~1003 K, ~25 K variation) followed by rapid microsecond-timescale melt-quenching; transitions are typically thermally driven via Joule heating, and partially amorphized intermediate states behave as solid–liquid dielectric composites, with permittivity modeled from solid- and liquid-state dielectric constants and the local amorphization fraction. At 1550 nm it exhibits refractive indices of 2.85 (amorphous) and 3.2 (crystalline) with nearly zero absorption, offering low insertion loss in both states, low mid-wave-infrared loss, and an advantage over PCMs such as GST for C-band telecom applications.

Beyond phase-change behavior, Sb2S3 exhibits weak ferroelectricity alongside notable piezoelectric and dielectric characteristics; its nanoscale ferroelectric properties and electromechanical behavior, including electromechanical hysteresis, are highly responsive, with polarization switching drivable by the excitation waveform. Its structural, vibrational, and phase-transition properties have been investigated, including under gigapascal pressures, and crystals have been formed on Sb-S-I glass by laser induction.

In device work, Sb2S3 is studied in thin-film form—typically sputtered as a 40 nm layer capped with 80 nm Al2O3 and crystallized by rapid thermal annealing at 325 °C for 10 minutes, or deposited at 55 nm thickness on waveguides. It serves as an electrically switchable PCM in reconfigurable photonic and thermal devices on platforms such as InP and silicon nitride, spanning varifocal metalenses and hybrid-integrated external-cavity lasers combining it with silicon nitride sidewall Bragg gratings, where its high refractive-index contrast (the crystalline phase pulling the optical field upward) enables non-volatile, reconfigurable C-band wavelength tuning (~0.8 nm channel spacing) as an alternative to thermo-optic tuning. Pixel/device-level switching has been achieved with conventional and inverse-designed heaters—including inverse-designed doped-Si heaters enabling dynamic thermal emission control, with a large (~18×14 μm²) area switched by a compact 26×26 μm² heater—and via voltage pulses applied to Pd electrodes, with crystallinity adjusted through electrical pulse duration. Joule heating may raise active-region temperature, altering the effective index and degrading single-mode operation. Characterization techniques include Piezoresponse Force Microscopy (PFM) for nanoscale electromechanical behavior and polarization switching, and photoelectron spectroscopy for crystal structure and surface properties."}
{"entity": "Phase Change Materials", "type": "artifact", "description": ""Phase Change Materials" (PCMs) refers to two distinct classes of materials that share the same name: (1) thermal energy storage materials used in thermal management engineering, and (2) chalcogenide semiconductors used in electronic, photonic, and data storage applications.

In thermal engineering, Phase Change Materials are substances with a high latent heat of fusion that melt and solidify within a narrow temperature range while storing and releasing substantial amounts of heat. Examples include paraffin waxes, whose melting behavior and phase change characteristics underpin their thermal energy storage capability. These materials are employed for passive thermal management of batteries in electric vehicles, regulating battery thermal behavior and extending heat holding capacity. Implementation approaches include PCM encapsulation pouches, pure encapsulation designs, hybrid liquid cooling configurations, and combinations with heat pipe technology for hybrid thermal management of lithium-ion batteries, with passive approaches studied in contrast to active and hybrid cooling methods. PCMs are also used as passive cooling solutions for photovoltaic systems, absorbing heat and providing temperature drops without requiring energy input, and they find broader application in solar PV cooling, battery management, and green buildings.

In materials science and electronics, Phase Change Materials are a special class of semiconductor materials, most commonly composed of chalcogenide alloys such as GST-124 and GST-225, that can be reversibly switched between amorphous and crystalline phases. These thermally activated transitions are typically induced by laser pulses or controlled thermal pulses. Their exceptional optical and electrical contrast between phases makes them valuable for encoding and rewriting information in data storage, memory devices, and computing applications, as well as in photonics and nanophotonics—including optical switching, the subject of dedicated theses and reports—where their optical properties can be locally modified. PCMs undergo crystallization involving nucleation and crystal growth, and are sub-classified as nucleation-dominated or growth-dominated based on their dominant crystallization mechanism. Crystallization kinetics, cyclability, and the asymmetric crystallization window are central to their switching dynamics. Metavalent bonding plays a central role in this class, and telluride-based compounds exhibit structures amenable to fast phase transitions. These materials are studied using first-principles methods, through which new ternary compositions are predicted from binary parent compounds; additionally, confining heat within the material enhances thermal efficiency and reduces the energy required for phase transition.

"Phase Change Materials" is also the title of a review article by Simone Raoux, published as a book by Springer US in 2009, and cited in the literature (e.g., as reference 28 in source documents)."}
