{"entity": "Hysteresis", "type": "concept", "description": "Hysteresis is a path-dependent physical phenomenon in which a system's response depends on its prior history, such that forward and reverse transitions do not retrace the same path. It is observed across a variety of experimental contexts, including phase-change materials, electrical transport, and microscopy.

In phase-change materials (PCMs), hysteresis manifests as the discrepancy between melting and solidification behavior, observable in both the peak temperatures and the phase-change range. This effect is particularly relevant for organic bio-based PCMs, being more pronounced in esters than in paraffin, as noted in prior studies. It also influences the effective activation window during thermal cycling of lauric/myristic acid eutectics. Quantitatively, peak temperature shifts of 2.63 °C (P21), 3.52 °C (E21), and 4.09 °C (E19) have been reported, and in certain materials melting consistently exceeds the crystallization temperature by 4.2–11.1 °C, with the largest gap occurring at the low-temperature peak of MS5-25-MK. These hysteretic characteristics are typically analyzed alongside the steady-state thermal constants of the materials.

In electrical transport phenomena, hysteresis appears as the irreversibility of crystallization on cooling: when a sample is cooled below the crystallization temperature (T_crys), its resistance does not follow the same linear law observed during heating. Similarly, threshold switching exhibits a hysteretic nature, in which the ON state persists even as the voltage decreases back toward the holding regime, in contrast to the linear response characteristic of the OFF state.

In Piezoresponse Force Microscopy (PFM) experiments, hysteresis is treated as a measurable physical phenomenon, quantified by measuring the loop area across a microscopic sample surface, thereby providing spatially resolved characterization of the effect."}
