{"entity": "Phase Change Properties of PCMs (P21, E21, E19) from LT-DSC Tests (table)", "type": "table", "description": "Table 2 reports LT-DSC-derived phase change properties—latent heat, peak temperature, and phase change range—for paraffin P21 and esters E21/E19, during melting and solidification. Latent heats cluster near 200 kJ/kg (191.29–209.52). P21 shows the broadest range (~11.4–11.6°C) versus esters (4.44–7.57°C). Solidification peaks sit 2.63–4.09°C below melting peaks, evidencing hysteresis, strongest in esters. Peak temperatures near 16.7–22.8°C justify these PCMs for thermally inertial building mortars."}
{"entity": "Crystalline Sb2S3", "type": "naturalobject", "description": "Crystalline Sb2S3, also known as crystalline antimony trisulfide, is a specific structural phase of the chemical compound characterized by its Van der Waals structure and low defect formation energies. It is identified as a stiffer phase that exhibits higher contact resonance frequencies and behaves functionally as a conductor. Furthermore, this material phase demonstrates a significantly lower surface potential, measuring approximately -100 to -120 mV.

This crystalline state is highly notable for its strongly nonlinear electromechanical properties, dynamic induced polarization, and unique dynamic surface charging behavior, exhibiting characteristics akin to ferroelectric or ferroionic materials. Researchers actively investigate Crystalline Sb2S3 to determine the exact mechanisms driving its electromechanical coupling and hysteresis—specifically, whether these phenomena arise primarily from ferroelectric polarization, chemical strain, or surface charging. Supporting these properties is an additional local, time-dependent electrostatic response with a positive coefficient; this involves a dynamic surface charging mechanism that slowly screens an applied voltage with a relaxation time of approximately 0.2 seconds.

In Piezoresponse Force Microscopy (PFM) experiments, Crystalline Sb2S3 demonstrates a highly prominent electromechanical response. This response is specifically characterized by a wing-like symmetric and unsaturated hysteresis loop, which produces significant loop area values. When subjected to electrical stressing or a large direct current (DC) bias, the material develops a large, hysteretic PFM response. This application of bias also induces permanent physical changes to the material's surface topology, resulting in the formation of distinct dimpled protrusions.

In experimental visualizations, such as spatial heat maps, the Crystalline Sb2S3 phase is distinctly represented and easily identifiable. In the Pristine section of these visual mappings, it appears slightly darker and possesses a more negative potential, characteristically located to the left of a diagonal line. Within the spatial heat maps, this phase typically occupies the lower portion and covers the larger remaining area of Panel B, where its regions are distinctly marked by a predominantly dark blue or purple hue."}
