{"entity": "Phase Change Material", "type": "artifact", "description": "Phase Change Material (PCM) is a class of functional materials capable of storing and releasing large amounts of thermal energy as latent heat during phase transitions—most commonly between solid and liquid states—while maintaining a nearly constant temperature. PCMs encompass organic, inorganic, hydrated salt, fatty acid, and eutectic variants, with examples including paraffin wax, paraffin oil, palm wax, RT27, RT75, and composite formulations such as PCM-B-CRT27; a eutectic inorganic-organic PCM exhibits a melting range of 95.25–105.04°C and a specific heat capacity of 9.47 J/g.

In buildings, PCMs are integrated into envelopes, walls, roofs, glazing, facades, and roofing systems for dynamic heat storage and release, providing thermal buffering, reducing cooling loads, and improving comfort and energy efficiency. They are implemented in encapsulated, shape-stabilized, and macro-/micro-encapsulated forms, as well as in 3D-printed concrete, cement-lime mortars, gypsum, and porous calcium silicate panels. In cement-lime pastes, PCM reduces thermal conductivity and open porosity while substantially enhancing latent heat storage (e.g., melting point 23°C). In 3D-printed partitions, PCM content and form significantly enhance heat accumulation, though effects emerge only beyond a concentration threshold; cost remains a barrier to adoption in hot-humid climates.

In photovoltaics, PCMs provide passive cooling in PV-PCM, PVT, and BIPVT systems, absorbing excess heat during melting to regulate temperature and mitigate efficiency losses (e.g., reducing cell temperature by 4.3 K when used alone; a 307 K melting point can cause elevated nighttime temperatures during solidification). Paraffin-based PCM packs attached to BIPVT modules stabilize panel temperature and store recovered thermal energy, although direct attachment to frameless curtain-wall modules reduced thermal performance by 14.2% in one study.

In electronics thermal management, PCM-based heat sinks exploit latent heat absorption; typical properties include thermal conductivity of ~0.2 W/m·K, latent heat of ~200 kJ/kg, and densities of 860 (liquid) and ~900 (solid) kg/m³. PCMs can maintain device temperatures within ±5°C, lower maxima by 10–70°C, and extend thermal regulation periods. Melting behavior—modeled via enthalpy-porosity and lattice Boltzmann methods—involves buoyancy-driven natural convection, and fins, metal foams, and nanoparticles are employed to overcome inherently low thermal conductivity.

Further applications include battery thermal management (absorbing latent heat during discharge, improved with additives and encapsulation), cold storage and coolboxes for preserving food and fisheries products (pre-charged at −19°C and outperforming ice), solar drying systems enabling operation beyond sunlight hours, asphalt mixtures mitigating urban heat islands, temperature-controlled packaging, and micro-cold storage units. Separately, a distinct PCM class—exemplified by Ge2Sb2Te5—switches between amorphous and crystalline phases, with switching kinetics analyzed via Time-Temperature-Transformation diagrams; mixtures that phase-segregate upon crystallization are considered poor PCMs."}
{"entity": "Sb2S3 Nanowires", "type": "naturalobject", "description": "Nanowires of Sb2S3 whose hysteretic behavior closely matches the shape observed in Figure 2d."}
{"entity": "Phase Change Materials (PCM)", "type": "artifact", "description": "Phase Change Materials (PCM) are a class of substances that absorb or release large amounts of latent heat during phase transitions—typically from solid to liquid—enabling thermal energy storage, passive thermal regulation, and temperature stabilization. Effective performance requires specific thermodynamic, chemical, economic, and kinetic properties, with thermal characteristics such as melting temperature, latent heat of fusion, and specific heat capacity routinely quantified. PCM encompass organic, inorganic, and eutectic compositions; eutectic combinations with desirable thermal properties have been explored for coolbox applications, and the materials serve in passive latent thermal storage systems.

In building and construction, PCM are widely applied for thermal energy storage, thermal comfort, and energy efficiency. They are employed through encapsulation and integration into building elements, incorporated into retrofit projects in configurations varying by melting point, thickness, and placement within walls, roofs, or glazing, integrated into building envelopes in factorial studies analyzing their thermal properties, and used in facades to influence indoor thermal comfort. They can also be integrated into concrete to provide thermal regulation by absorbing and releasing energy. PCM are incorporated into mortars in different typologies and formats—including form-stable, slurry-microencapsulated, and powder-microencapsulated types used to enhance lime mortars in sustainable buildings—with their influence on mortar mechanical performance assessed. Additional uses include passive cooling and thermal regulation of ceilings, walls, and interior spaces.

Beyond buildings, PCM are employed for passive battery thermal management and for cooling photovoltaic (PV) panels, enhancing power output and electrical efficiency, with different types of varying melting points applied for thermal storage and temperature regulation. They also feature in heat exchanger applications, contributing to thermal comfort and reduced energy consumption, and serve as base materials into which nanoparticles are dispersed to create nano-enhanced phase change materials for thermal energy storage.

PCM are the subject of extensive academic research, constituting the central topic of a source document and of Perihan ÇULUN's presentation (16:35–16:45) in SESSION II of PCFM2026. Studies address enthalpy determination, phase change hysteresis, and supercooling, and experimentally examine melting and solidification thermal performance. In one experimental setup, PCM were contained within the lower vessel, with heat absorption capacity affected by the container's aspect ratio; in another, a central rectangular region of a duct Test Section was designated for PCM to study thermal discharging performance. Their thermo-physical properties have additionally undergone uncertainty propagation and sensitivity analysis in energy demand calculations.<SEP>Passive heat-buffering materials providing significant latent heat capacity for photovoltaic cooling; organic PCMs have low intrinsic thermal conductivity and can lose cooling capacity when fully melted."}
