{"entity1": "Phase-Change Material", "entity2": "Sb2S3", "description": "The relation (Phase-Change Material, Sb2S3) denotes that Sb2S3, also known as antimony trisulfide, is classified as a phase-change material (PCM). Sb2S3 is specifically a chalcogenide phase-change material distinguished by its nonvolatile phase-transition characteristics, enabling switching between amorphous and crystalline states accompanied by a pronounced refractive-index contrast. A notable property of this material is its low optical loss in the mid-wave infrared region, making it particularly well-suited for photonic applications in that spectral range.

Within the associated research contexts, Sb2S3 functions as the electrically switchable phase-change material employed for switching operations in experimental implementations. It is leveraged to deliver non-volatile, fast, and reconfigurable tuning, serving as an alternative to conventional thermo-optic phase shifter solutions. Owing to its high refractive-index contrast, Sb2S3 enables effective modulation of optical properties without requiring continuous power to maintain a given state, which underpins its non-volatile operational behavior.

Applications of Sb2S3 highlighted in these contexts include tunable wavelength control in lasers, where it provides the mechanism for adjusting emission characteristics; non-volatile and reconfigurable wavelength tuning; and stable resonance tuning in photonic structures. Collectively, these descriptions establish Sb2S3 as a versatile phase-change material whose combination of non-volatile switching, refractive-index contrast, fast response, low loss in the mid-wave infrared, and electrical switchability makes it a valuable component for reconfigurable photonic devices and tunable laser systems."}
