An advanced superlattice $\left( \mathrm { G e T e / T i  – S b _ { 2 } T e _ { 3 } } \right)$ was investigated during a study in 2023 by Lim et al.. After electrical pulsing, they found that the internal structure of the SL-device was well maintained. This advanced superlattice is characterized by an ultra-low RESET energy $( \sim 2 \mathrm { \ p J ) }$ , high switching speed $( \sim 8 ~ \mathrm { n s } )$ , and high cycling endurance $( \sim 3 * 1 0 ^ { 9 } ) . [ 2 1 ]$

High SL-quality is essential for achieving the desired electrical and structural properties that enable energy-eficient switching. One important indicator of $_ { \mathrm { S L - q u a l i t y } }$ is the presence of well-defined interfaces with van der Waals (vdW)-like gaps between adjacent layers. For GeTe/Sb<sub>2</sub>Te<sub>3</sub> superlattices, previous studies have shown that intermixing between GeTe and $\mathrm { S b _ { 2 } T e _ { 3 } }$ thin layers occurs during deposition at the elevated temperatures required for epitaxial growth.[22, 23] Instead of forming the desired superlattice, a heterostructure consisting of $\mathrm { S b _ { 2 } T e _ { 3 } }$ and stable GeSbTe (GST) is formed due to thermodynamically driven intermixing at the interfaces.[24]

In this work, high-quality $\mathrm { G e _ { 1 } S b _ { 2 } T e _ { 4 } / I n _ { 2 } T e _ { 3 } }$ superlattices are investigated, which were grown by Lucas Bothe at the Helmholtz Nano Facility (HNF) at Forschungszentrum Jülich (FZJ).

