Analysis: This figure presents a Band Excitation Polarization Switching PFM (Piezoresponse Force Microscopy) analysis of Sb2S3 glass-ceramic, organized as a four-panel composite (a-d). Panel (a) shows a spatial map of PFM amplitude (scale ~0-3 pm) before DC bias application. Panel (b) displays the corresponding PFM phase map (scale ~-140° to +140°). Panel (c) presents the hysteresis loop area map, calculated by integrating PFM signal over 4 DC bias cycles (±30V at 0.87 Hz), with scale ~0-15 pm·V, revealing sharp contrast between crystalline and glassy regions. Panel (d) plots averaged off-field PFM hysteresis loops from k-means clustering showing two clusters representing crystalline Sb2S3 (showing wing-like symmetric hysteretic response) versus glassy Sb2S3 (showing small non-hysteretic response), with an inset showing the spatial distribution of clusters. The figure is central to the paper's argument that crystalline Sb2S3 exhibits hysteretic surface polarization distinct from ferroelectric behavior, with loop shapes resembling those of electrochemically active materials (NiO, SrTiO3, TiO2) known for resistive switching and filament formation. The findings support subsequent cKPFM analysis to differentiate ferroelectric from non-ferroelectric effects, and connect to KPFM observations of surface protrusions after bias application."}
