Analysis: This table (Table 2) presents the phase change properties of three phase change materials—paraffin P21 and two bio-based esters E21 and E19—derived from Low-Temperature Differential Scanning Calorimetry (LT-DSC) tests, as referenced in Section 3.1 of the study on thermally enhanced mortars.

**Structure and organization:** The table uses a two-tier column header: the top tier groups three thermodynamic properties (Latent heat in kJ/kg, Peak temperature in °C, and Phase change range in °C), each subdivided into three columns for the three PCMs (P21, E21, E19). The rows separate the two directions of the phase transition: Melting and Solidification, enabling direct comparison for hysteresis analysis.

**Key data points:**
- Latent heat is remarkably uniform across all materials, spanning 191.29–209.52 kJ/kg. E19 exhibits the highest values (205.79 kJ/kg melting; 209.52 kJ/kg solidification), while E21 shows the lowest solidification value (191.29 kJ/kg). This confirms the text's statement that 'the latent heat of all PCMs is similar, around 200 kJ/kg.'
- Peak temperatures for melting range narrowly from 20.82°C (E19) to 22.84°C (P21), and solidification peaks from 16.73°C (E19) to 20.21°C (P21)—all near room/comfort temperature, which is precisely why these PCMs were selected for building thermal inertia applications.
- Phase change range widths during melting: P21 is broadest at 11.39°C (13.58–24.97°C), versus 7.57°C for E21 (18.14–25.71°C) and 6.93°C for E19 (16.51–23.44°C)—matching the text's cited values of ~11.5°C, 7.57°C, and 6.93°C, and consistent with the bell-shaped DSC curves in Fig. 4.

**Statistical insights and hysteresis quantification:** The melting-to-solidification discrepancies quantify the hysteresis effect discussed in the text. Peak temperature shifts are 2.63°C for P21, 3.52°C for E21, and 4.09°C for E19, with solidification consistently occurring at lower temperatures (notably explaining E21's subcooling behavior visible in Fig. 4). Phase change range width discrepancies between melting and solidification are modest for P21 (11.39°C vs. 11.64°C) but pronounced for the esters: E21 contracts from 7.57°C to 4.44°C (a 3.13°C difference) and E19 from 6.93°C to 5.44°C. Latent heat discrepancies follow a similar pattern (5.75 kJ/kg for P21 versus 10.36 kJ/kg for E21). These figures substantiate the paper's claim, citing prior studies [57], that hysteresis is more significant in organic bio-based PCMs (the esters) than in paraffin.

**Relationships and significance to surrounding context:** The table numerically consolidates the qualitative DSC curve analysis (Fig. 4), converting heat flow curves into actionable selection parameters—latent heat (energy storage capacity), peak temperature (operating point), and phase change range (thermal buffering window). It establishes the material baseline before the mortars (NAM, RCAM, LWAM) are impregnated: all three PCMs store comparable energy per kilogram (~200 kJ/kg), so differences in mortar performance later in the study will stem primarily from incorporation volume (V_PCM), viscosity (P21 at 12.6 mPa·s vs. esters at 5.2–6 mPa·s), and thermal effusivity (Table 3) rather than latent heat. The peak temperatures of 16.7–22.8°C align with indoor building temperatures, directly supporting the study's goal of enhancing thermal inertia and stabilizing indoor climates through PCM-modified mortars."}
