Analysis: Figure 5 presents an in-contact dynamic electromechanical experiment conducted on Sb2S3 thin films, consisting of three panels (a, b, c) that characterize the time-dependent electromechanical response under pulsed DC bias. Panel (a) displays the electromechanical response (D_ac) as a function of time for both crystalline and glassy Sb2S3 phases, revealing distinct relaxation behaviors between the two phases. Panel (b) shows the corresponding applied bias waveform as a function of time, consisting of DC pulses of ±30V used to polarize the sample surface. Panel (c) presents spatial mapping of the pre-exponential factor from stretched exponential fits to the transient D_ac response following +30V pulses, providing quantitative analysis of relaxation dynamics across the scanned area. This figure directly supports the paper's central finding that the measured 'PFM signal' in Sb2S3 is dominated by electrostatic forces and surface charging rather than true piezoelectric/ferroelectric response. The crystalline phase exhibits an additional local, time-dependent component with a positive coefficient where surface charging slowly screens the applied V_dc, with a relaxation time on the order of ~0.2 seconds. In contrast, glassy Sb2S3 behaves as an insulating dielectric with only a fast/instantaneous electrostatic component. The stark contrast between phases—despite similar chemistry—rules out surface adsorbed species as the origin and points to bulk-derived differences, likely related to the Van der Waals crystal structure and low defect formation energies in crystalline Sb2S3. This characterization distinguishes glassy Sb2S3 as an insulating dielectric and crystalline Sb2S3 as a conductor, with implications for neuromorphic computing applications utilizing dynamic electrical behavior on millisecond-to-second timescales."}
{"reference_id": "9", "content": "Mathematical Equation Analysis:
Equation: $$
\begin{array}{l} \text {Annual operational energy savings (S)} \\ = (\text {Base case energy consumption} \\ - \text {PCM scenario energy consumption}) \end{array}
$$
Format: latex

Mathematical Analysis: This equation defines the annual operational energy savings (S) as the arithmetic difference between the energy consumption of a baseline building model (without PCM integration) and the energy consumption of a given PCM-enhanced scenario. Mathematically, it is expressed as a linear subtraction: S = E_base − E_PCM, where E_base represents the annual energy consumption (typically quantified in kWh or kWh/m² per year) of the reference building configuration, and E_PCM represents the corresponding annual energy consumption after Phase Change Material integration into building envelope components (walls, roofs, or glazing assemblies). The equation operates within the EnergyPlus simulation engine framework, where DesignBuilder software computes annual operational energy demand for each of the 10 tested scenarios against a validated baseline of 190.07 kWh/m²/year (validated to within 2.05% of Friess et al.'s measured EUI of 194 kWh/m²). The variable S serves as a critical input to downstream financial calculations, specifically feeding into the Net Present Value (NPV) formulation where S represents the annual savings at time t, discounted over the 35-year PCM service life at a 4.35% discount rate. The equation embodies the fundamental performance metric of the technoeconomic analysis, establishing the quantitative foundation upon which cost-effectiveness, payback period determination, and sensitivity analyses are constructed. By isolating the differential energy performance attributable to PCM thermal storage behavior—characterized through enthalpy-temperature hysteresis modeling—this difference operator enables direct comparison across PCM configurations varying by melting point (RT31, RT47, RT55), thickness (3 cm vs. 5 cm), and placement location (interior walls, exterior roofs, glazing cavities). The simplicity of this first-order difference equation belies its strategic importance: it transforms complex thermodynamic phase-change phenomena—including latent heat absorption/release, diurnal thermal buffering, and temperature-dependent thermal conductivity variations—into a single, monetizable scalar quantity suitable for life cycle cost assessment and investment decision-making in hot-humid climate contexts such as the UAE."}
