{"entity": "Phase-Change Materials", "type": "concept", "description": "Phase-Change Materials (PCMs) are a class of materials whose properties change markedly and reversibly between phases. The term spans two largely distinct research domains: chalcogenide-based electronic and optical switching materials used in information technology, and latent-heat thermal energy storage materials used in thermal engineering.

In electronics, photonics, and data storage, PCMs are chalcogenide semiconductors and glasses—such as GeTe, Ge₂Sb₂Te₅ (GST), Ge–Sb–Te alloys, AgIn-doped Sb₂Te, Ge₃Sn₁Te₄, and Ge₂Sn₂Te₄—that reversibly switch between amorphous and crystalline phases. The two phases exhibit pronounced contrasts in resistivity (differing by orders of magnitude; amorphous values roughly 10–2000 Ωcm), optical reflectivity, and dielectric constant; both remain stable at room temperature for decades, while switching occurs within nanoseconds at elevated temperatures, with electronic changes even faster. Thermodynamically, the crystalline state is the global free-energy minimum, whereas the amorphous phase and certain crystalline polymorphs are metastable minima separated by activation barriers. Crystallization proceeds via nucleation and growth, with the critical radius governing kinetics, SET speed, and data retention. The crystalline phase exhibits resonant bonding—unusually high resistivity despite lacking conventional band gaps—and many PCMs fall within the metavalent bonding region, making PMI/PME analysis important since local atomic structure and bonding determine optical and electrical contrast. Threshold switching is modeled by the Adler–Pirovano purely electronic framework; Krebs determined threshold fields and a linear correlation between threshold voltage and layer thickness. Amorphous resistance drift, activation-energy drift, and logarithmic structural relaxation are captured by drift laws such as Boniardi's, with materials classified as Ge₂Sb₂Te₅-like (capable of negative β) or GeTe-like. These properties underpin rewritable optical storage (CD-RW, DVD-RW, Blu-ray), phase-change random access memory, and storage-class memory; in mushroom-type cells, PCMs are mechanically less robust and more resistive than TiN. Research—dating to 1968, with Yamada's 1987 work a landmark—covers defect and vacancy engineering, superlattices, electric-field-driven crystallization, gigahertz-bandwidth and sub-nanosecond switching measurements (e.g., Pulsed Electrical Testers), nanophotonic tuning of nanoantenna resonances, GeTe–SnTe pseudobinary optimization, and data-driven materials discovery, with M. Wuttig a prominent researcher and Gunnar Bruns's doctoral work on electronic switching notable. PCMs also serve as the active tuning medium in electrically tunable metasurfaces, as the switching medium in inverse-designed microheaters, and—owing to large thermo-optic coefficients—in smart optical materials and thermo-optical switching research.

In thermal engineering, PCMs absorb and release latent heat during phase transitions such as melting and solidification, making them central to latent-heat thermal energy storage. Alkanes and paraffin waxes are typical organic PCMs, and paraffin-based composites—the central subject of the associated cited article and parent paper—are widely studied. Their characteristically low intrinsic thermal conductivity, a particular limitation of organic PCMs, is mitigated with conductive fillers such as graphite; form-stable and shape-stabilized composites are extensively evaluated through thermal, morphological, and elemental analysis. Applications include integration into building facades to moderate temperature fluctuations for vulnerable, temperature-sensitive populations, and microencapsulation within biosourced shells for lithium-ion battery thermal management.<SEP>A technology based on materials exhibiting phase-change properties used for thermal energy storage applications, which underpins the composite material analyzed in the cited article.<SEP>Class of materials exhibiting reversible amorphous/crystalline phase transitions that support non-volatile state changes and multiple refractive-index states for tunable photonic functionality."}
