3.1. Laser-induced switching of Phase Change Superlattices

The reversible switching process between the amorphous and crystalline phases can be induced either electrically (via Joule heating) or optically. In this work, local optical switching is achieved using laser pulses. The underlying physics of this process is governed by the phase-transition kinetics described by the time-temperaturetransformation (TTT) diagram (Fig. 2.8). Amorphization is achieved by heating the crystalline material above $\mathrm { T } _ { L } .$ , followed by rapid quenching of the melt below $\mathrm { T } _ { C } .$ resulting in a disordered amorphous state. This requires relatively high-energy laser pulses with very short pulse durations in the nanosecond regime.

In contrast, crystallization is induced by lower-energy laser pulses with longer durations, allowing suficient time for atomic rearrangement into the energetically favorable crystalline state. Only carefully controlled laser parameters that heat the amorphous material to temperatures between $\mathrm { T } _ { C }$ and $\mathrm { T } _ { L }$ enable crystallization.[25]

In her Master’s thesis, Athanasia Piperidou performed optical switching experiments of $\mathrm { S b _ { 2 } T e _ { 3 } / G e _ { 1 } S b _ { 2 } T e _ { 4 } }$ superlattices and observed amorphization at a laser power of 170 mW with a pulse duration of 16 ns. Optimal recrystallization was achieved after 20 laser pulses using a lower power of approximately 50 mW and a pulse duration of approximately 1800 ns.[10] This is relevant for the present work, as the switching experiments were performed using the same setup, allowing her previously determined optimal parameters to define an appropriate range of laser conditions for the samples investigated in this study.

3.1.1. Laser Switching Setup (LSS)

The experimental setup used to perform the switching experiments is the Laser Switching Setup (LSS) depicted in Fig. 3.2. Well-controlled laser pulses with adjustable parameters enable the local optical switching of the PCM superlattices investigated in this work.

The laser beam is generated by a semiconductor pulsed laser diode driven by a PICOLAS module (PLCS-21), emitting at a wavelength of $\lambda = 6 6 0 \mathrm { n m }$ . The PLCS-21 acts as a digital pulse generator with adjustable laser pulse parameters and is controlled via a connected PC. It converts an applied voltage U at the laser diode into an average pulse power

with a maximum output power of 400 mW. Using a LabVIEW-based control program, four key parameters characterizing the laser pulses can be adjusted: the pulse duration $\tau \ ( 1 { - } 1 0 0 0 0 \mathrm { n s } )$ , the pulse power (0–150 mW for $\tau < 3 0$ ns and 0–400 mW for $\tau > 3 0 \mathrm { n s } )$ , the repetition rate $f \ ( 1 { - } 2 0 0 0 \mathrm { { H z } ) }$ , and the number of pulses.

The laser beam is initially collimated using an aspheric lens (NA = 0.5) and is then guided via two mirrors towards a dichroic beam combiner. As shown in the upper part of Fig. 3.2, a white LED beam is coupled into the optical path via reflection at a beam splitter. The laser beam path is indicated in red, while the LED path is shown in blue. The dichroic combiner reflects the laser wavelength while transmitting the LED light, thereby aligning both optical paths.

After passing through a microscope objective (10×, NA = 0.25), both beams are focused onto the surface of the sample, which is mounted on a stage using a sample holder. The reflected light from the sample is collected by the same objective, transmitted through the beam splitter, and guided towards a CCD camera, enabling real-time visualization of the sample surface and precise positioning of the laser spot using IDS software on the connected PC.

The stage is a high-precision NanoMax-TS piezoelectric positioning stage (Thorlabs GmbH), which allows movement in all three spatial dimensions with nanometerscale precision. It is controlled via the LabVIEW-based program and provides a positioning range of $2 0 \mu \mathrm { m }$ with a step accuracy of 5 nm.[10, 25]

3.2. Characterization techniques

To identify the phases and evaluate the quality of possible switching parameters, several characterization methods can be employed to confirm local amorphization and crystallization.

3.2.1. Optical Microscopy (OM)

One of the key diferences between the amorphous and crystalline phases is their optical reflectivity. Crystalline regions exhibit high reflectivity and therefore appear bright, whereas amorphous regions, due to their lower reflectivity, appear dark in optical microscopy. This contrast enables a clear spatial separation of amorphous and crystalline domains.

In this work, a Keyence VHX-7000 optical microscope was used at a maximum magnification of 2000×, allowing for detailed visualization of the regions of interest. The built-in functionality for measuring the area of regions with distinct brightness contrast was the primary reason this microscope was particularly well suited for this study. This feature enables the distinction between amorphous and crystalline regions.

